  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately describes the benefits of version control for IaC. By storing infrastructure configurations in systems like Git, teams can track every change, collaborate effectively through branching and merging, and easily revert to a previous working state if issues arise. Option B (incorrect) describes continuous deployment, which is enabled by IaC but not the sole purpose of version control itself. Option C (incorrect) describes a security feature, not a function of version control. Option D (incorrect) is a potential outcome of optimized IaC, but not a direct function of version control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_2_32',
  'Which benefit of Infrastructure as Code (IaC) helps prevent ''configuration drift'' where environments diverge from their intended state?',
  'mcq',
  '["Consistency and repeatability of deployments.","Reduced cloud billing costs.","Enhanced application performance.","Automatic code generation for new services."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is a direct benefit that prevents configuration drift. By defining infrastructure in code, IaC ensures that environments are consistently deployed according to the specified configuration. If manual changes occur, IaC tools like Terraform can detect this drift and allow operators to revert or update the infrastructure to match the desired state. Option B (incorrect) is not a direct or guaranteed benefit of IaC; while IaC can help manage costs, it doesn''t automatically reduce them. Option C (incorrect) is an application-level concern, not a direct benefit of IaC. Option D (incorrect) is false; IaC requires writing configurations, not automatic code generation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
