INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_16',
  'Terraform is primarily considered a declarative Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool. What does ''declarative'' mean in this context?',
  'mcq',
  '["You specify the exact sequence of commands Terraform must execute to build infrastructure.","You define the desired end state of your infrastructure, and Terraform figures out how to achieve it.","You declare infrastructure resources using programming languages like Python or Java.","Terraform declares its own operational state to a central management server."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately defines a declarative approach. With Terraform, you describe the desired state of your infrastructure (e.g., ''I want an Amazon S3 bucket with these properties''), and Terraform determines the necessary steps to reach that state. Option A is incorrect and represents a common misconception (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001); an imperative approach specifies the sequence of commands. Option C is incorrect; Terraform uses its own HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL). Option D is unrelated to the definition of declarative IaC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_17',
  'Which property of Infrastructure as Code (IaC) ensures that applying the same configuration multiple times will always result in the same infrastructure state, without unintended side effects?',
  'mcq',
  '["Volatility","Transience","Idempotency","Variability"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the definition of idempotency. This property is crucial for IaC, as it allows configurations to be applied repeatedly without fear of breaking existing infrastructure or creating duplicate resources. Options A, B, and D are incorrect terms and do not describe this specific property of IaC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_18',
  'What is the key difference between a declarative and an imperative approach to infrastructure provisioning?',
  'mcq',
  '["Declarative approaches use visual diagrams, while imperative approaches use text files.","Declarative approaches define the desired end state, while imperative approaches specify step-by-step commands.","Imperative approaches are always faster than declarative approaches.","Declarative approaches are only used for on-premises infrastructure, while imperative approaches are for cloud."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) captures the core distinction. A declarative approach (like Terraform) focuses on ''what'' the infrastructure should look like, letting the tool figure out ''how'' to achieve it. An imperative approach focuses on ''how'' to achieve the state, by providing a sequence of commands. Option A is incorrect; both can use text files. Option C is incorrect; the speed depends on many factors, not inherently the approach. Option D is incorrect; both approaches can be used for cloud or on-premises.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_19',
  'A system administrator writes a shell script that first creates an Amazon EC2 instance, then installs a web server, and finally starts the service. This approach is an example of what type of infrastructure provisioning?',
  'mcq',
  '["A declarative approach, as it defines the desired web server.","An idempotent approach, ensuring the same result every time.","An imperative approach, as it specifies the exact sequence of actions.","A stateful approach, managing the current state of the instance."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) describes this scenario. An imperative approach involves specifying the exact sequence of steps or commands that must be executed to achieve a desired configuration. The script explicitly dictates ''create, then install, then start.'' Option A is incorrect because a declarative approach would simply state ''I want a web server running'' and let the tool manage the steps. Option B is incorrect; while the script might be idempotent with careful design, the approach itself is imperative. Option D is a characteristic of how IaC tools manage infrastructure, not the provisioning approach itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_20',
  'Which of the following is a core principle of Infrastructure as Code (IaC) that treats infrastructure configurations similarly to application source code?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manual configuration via a web console.","Storing configurations in a version control system.","Avoiding any form of automation for infrastructure changes.","Keeping infrastructure configurations separate from any documentation."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is a core principle. Treating infrastructure configurations like application code means they should be stored in a version control system (like Git) to track changes, enable collaboration, and facilitate rollbacks. Option A describes an anti-pattern that IaC aims to replace. Option C is incorrect; IaC is fundamentally about automation. Option D is incorrect; IaC promotes ''documentation as code,'' where configurations themselves serve as documentation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_21',
  'A team uses Terraform to manage their AWS infrastructure. They run `terraform apply` multiple times on the same configuration without making changes to the code. What characteristic of Terraform ensures that the infrastructure remains unchanged after the first successful application?',
  'mcq',
  '["Imperativeness","Volatility","Idempotency","Transience"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the characteristic that ensures this behavior. Idempotency means that applying the same configuration multiple times will produce the same result as applying it once. Terraform achieves this by comparing the desired state (in code) with the actual state (in the cloud) and only making changes if a drift is detected. Option A (Imperativeness) is incorrect; Terraform is declarative. Options B (Volatility) and D (Transience) are incorrect terms in this context.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_22',
  'Which of the following best describes the core principle of Infrastructure as Code (IaC)?',
  'mcq',
  '["Managing infrastructure exclusively through manual console operations.","Representing and managing infrastructure configurations using code and automation.","Writing one-off scripts for every infrastructure change.","Delegating all infrastructure decisions to artificial intelligence."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) best describes the core principle of IaC. It involves treating infrastructure as software, defining it in code, and using automation to provision and manage it. Option A is the opposite of IaC. Option C describes a form of automation, but IaC emphasizes reusable, version-controlled definitions, not one-off scripts. Option D is beyond the current scope and capabilities of IaC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_23',
  'Which of the following statements accurately describes the declarative nature of Terraform?',
  'mcq',
  '["Terraform requires users to write scripts that specify the exact sequence of API calls.","Terraform allows users to define the desired state of their infrastructure, and it computes the necessary actions.","Terraform automatically discovers and documents existing infrastructure without user input.","Terraform primarily focuses on managing application code deployments, not infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes Terraform''s declarative nature. Users define the ''what'' (the desired state), and Terraform determines the ''how'' (the actions to achieve that state). Option A is incorrect and represents the anti-pattern ap-1.1-001, confusing declarative with imperative. Option C is incorrect; while Terraform can import existing resources, its primary function is provisioning based on defined configurations. Option D is incorrect; Terraform is an IaC tool, primarily focused on infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_24',
  'What is the main advantage of using a declarative IaC tool like Terraform over an imperative scripting approach for infrastructure management?',
  'mcq',
  '["Declarative tools require more manual intervention for changes.","Declarative tools focus on the desired end state, simplifying complex changes and reducing errors.","Imperative scripts are inherently more idempotent than declarative configurations.","Imperative scripts are easier to version control and audit."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) highlights the main advantage. Declarative tools like Terraform focus on describing the desired end state, making it easier to understand, manage, and evolve complex infrastructure configurations, and reducing the chance of errors from missed steps. Option A is incorrect; declarative tools aim to reduce manual intervention. Option C is incorrect; declarative tools are inherently designed for idempotency, while imperative scripts require careful design to achieve it. Option D is incorrect; both can be version controlled, but declarative configurations often provide a clearer, higher-level representation for auditing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_25',
  'What is a key characteristic of an idempotent operation in the context of Infrastructure as Code?',
  'mcq',
  '["It produces a different result each time it is executed.","It only works correctly on the first execution.","Applying it multiple times yields the same result as applying it once.","It requires manual verification after every execution."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) defines idempotency. In IaC, an idempotent operation ensures that if you apply the same configuration repeatedly, the infrastructure will consistently reach and remain in the desired state without creating duplicates or causing unintended changes. Option A and B are incorrect as they contradict the definition of idempotency. Option D is incorrect; while verification is good practice, it''s not a characteristic of idempotency itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_26',
  'What does the term ''idempotency'' mean in the context of Infrastructure as Code (IaC) with Terraform?',
  'mcq',
  '["Applying the same configuration multiple times will always result in the same desired infrastructure state.","Terraform configurations must be applied only once to avoid unintended resource duplication.","Infrastructure changes can only be performed by a single user at a time to maintain state consistency.","The process of converting existing manual infrastructure into Terraform configuration files."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately defines idempotency. In IaC, an idempotent operation means that applying the same configuration repeatedly will consistently achieve the same target state without causing unintended side effects or changes after the first successful application. Option B (incorrect) contradicts the principle of idempotency; Terraform is designed to be safely run multiple times. Option C (incorrect) describes a concurrency control mechanism, not idempotency. Option D (incorrect) refers to ''infrastructure import'' or ''state migration,'' which is a different concept from idempotency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_27',
  'Which statement best describes Terraform''s approach to infrastructure provisioning?',
  'mcq',
  '["Terraform uses a declarative approach, where users define the desired end state of their infrastructure.","Terraform uses an imperative approach, where users specify a sequence of commands to execute.","Terraform relies on manual API calls for resource creation and modification.","Terraform only supports provisioning virtual machines, not higher-level services."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately describes Terraform''s declarative nature. Users define ''what'' they want their infrastructure to look like (the desired end state), and Terraform determines ''how'' to achieve that state. Option B (incorrect) describes an imperative approach, which is contrary to Terraform''s design (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001). Option C (incorrect) is false; Terraform abstracts away direct API calls, managing them on behalf of the user. Option D (incorrect) is also false; Terraform supports a vast array of cloud services, including databases, networking, serverless functions, and more, beyond just virtual machines.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_28',
  'A team is managing a complex application environment using Terraform. They frequently apply changes to their infrastructure. What does Terraform''s declarative nature imply about these operations?',
  'mcq',
  '["The team defines the desired end state, and Terraform figures out the necessary steps to reach it.","The team must write explicit scripts detailing every API call Terraform should make.","Terraform will only apply changes if the infrastructure state is completely empty.","Terraform requires manual confirmation for every single resource modification."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately describes Terraform''s declarative approach. Users declare the desired state of their infrastructure, and Terraform''s execution plan engine determines the sequence of actions (create, update, delete) required to transition the current state to the desired state. Option B (incorrect) describes an imperative approach, which is the opposite of how Terraform operates (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001). Option C (incorrect) is false; Terraform can manage existing infrastructure and apply incremental changes. Option D (incorrect) is also false; while Terraform requires confirmation for `terraform apply` by default, it doesn''t require confirmation for *every single resource modification* within a plan, which would negate automation benefits.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_29',
  'What is the primary goal of Infrastructure as Code (IaC)?',
  'mcq',
  '["To manage and provision infrastructure through machine-readable definition files rather than manual processes.","To eliminate the need for any human interaction in infrastructure deployment.","To replace all existing cloud provider consoles with a single Terraform interface.","To ensure that all infrastructure is deployed as serverless functions."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) precisely defines Infrastructure as Code. The core idea is to define infrastructure using code, allowing for automation, version control, and consistent deployments, moving away from manual, error-prone processes. Option B (incorrect) is an overstatement; while IaC automates much, human interaction is still required for defining configurations, reviewing changes, and managing the IaC workflow. Option C (incorrect) is not the goal; Terraform integrates with existing cloud consoles and APIs, it doesn''t replace them entirely. Option D (incorrect) is false; IaC can manage any type of infrastructure, not just serverless functions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_30',
  'Which characteristic ensures that applying a Terraform configuration multiple times will always result in the same infrastructure state, without unintended side effects?',
  'mcq',
  '["Idempotency","Imperative execution","Declarative state","Version control"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the definition of idempotency. It means that an operation, when applied multiple times, produces the same result as if it were applied only once. Terraform''s design ensures its operations are idempotent. Option B (incorrect) refers to an approach where commands are executed in a specific order, which is not the characteristic ensuring consistent state on repeated applications. Option C (incorrect) refers to how Terraform defines the desired state, but ''idempotency'' is the property that ensures repeated applications of that declarative state yield the same outcome. Option D (incorrect) is a tool used with IaC for tracking changes, not a characteristic of the application process itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_31',
  'A DevOps engineer is asked to provision a new set of resources in an AWS account. Instead of manually clicking through the AWS Management Console, they write a Terraform configuration file. This approach is an example of:',
  'mcq',
  '["Infrastructure as Code (IaC).","Manual provisioning.","Imperative scripting.","Cloud-agnostic development."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the definition of Infrastructure as Code (IaC). Writing configuration files to provision and manage infrastructure instead of manual processes is the core principle of IaC. Option B (incorrect) is the opposite of the described approach. Option C (incorrect) is a type of programming paradigm, but the act of defining infrastructure in a configuration file is primarily IaC, and Terraform itself is declarative, not imperative (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001). Option D (incorrect) refers to designing applications that can run on any cloud, which is a broader concept than provisioning infrastructure with Terraform.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_32',
  'A developer writes a Terraform configuration to create an Amazon S3 bucket. They run `terraform apply` successfully. Later, they run `terraform apply` again without changing the configuration. What is the expected outcome due to Terraform''s idempotent nature?',
  'mcq',
  '["Terraform will report that no changes are needed, and the S3 bucket will remain in its existing state.","Terraform will attempt to create a new S3 bucket with the same name, resulting in an error.","Terraform will delete the existing S3 bucket and then recreate it.","Terraform will prompt the user for manual confirmation to skip the operation."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the expected outcome due to idempotency. If the desired state (defined in the configuration) matches the actual state (recorded in the state file and observed in the cloud), Terraform will determine that no actions are required and report ''no changes needed''. Option B (incorrect) would violate idempotency and likely result in an error due to unique naming constraints for S3 buckets. Option C (incorrect) would also violate idempotency and lead to unnecessary downtime and resource churn. Option D (incorrect) is false; while `terraform apply` requires confirmation by default, it would still show a plan of ''no changes'' before asking for confirmation if nothing needs to be done.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_33',
  'What does ''Infrastructure as Code'' (IaC) fundamentally mean?',
  'mcq',
  '["Managing and provisioning computing infrastructure using configuration files instead of manual processes.","Writing application code that automatically deploys itself to the cloud.","Using programming languages to define business logic for cloud applications.","Storing all operational logs and metrics in a centralized code repository."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the fundamental definition of IaC. It involves treating infrastructure definitions like software code, allowing for version control, automation, and consistent deployments. Option B (incorrect) describes continuous deployment for applications, not IaC. Option C (incorrect) describes application development, not infrastructure management. Option D (incorrect) describes a logging strategy, which is unrelated to the core concept of IaC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_34',
  'When using Terraform, you define the desired state of your infrastructure in HCL files. Terraform then determines the actions needed to achieve that state. This workflow exemplifies a(n) ________ approach.',
  'mcq',
  '["Declarative","Imperative","Procedural","Event-driven"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) describes Terraform''s declarative approach. You declare ''what'' you want the infrastructure to be, and Terraform figures out ''how'' to get there. Option B (incorrect) describes an imperative approach, where you specify a step-by-step sequence of commands to achieve a result (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001). Option C (incorrect) is similar to imperative, focusing on a sequence of steps or procedures. Option D (incorrect) describes a system that reacts to events, which is a different architectural pattern.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_35',
  'What is the primary difference between a declarative and an imperative approach to infrastructure provisioning?',
  'mcq',
  '["Declarative defines the desired end state, while imperative specifies the steps to reach that state.","Declarative uses graphical interfaces, while imperative uses command-line interfaces.","Declarative is only for on-premises, while imperative is for cloud environments.","Declarative is faster, while imperative is more secure."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately distinguishes between the two approaches. A declarative approach (like Terraform) focuses on ''what'' the desired state should be, letting the tool figure out the ''how''. An imperative approach focuses on ''how'' to achieve a state by defining a sequence of commands or steps. Option B (incorrect) is false; both can use CLI, and declarative tools like Terraform are CLI-based. Option C (incorrect) is false; both approaches can be used for both on-premises and cloud environments. Option D (incorrect) is a generalization that is not universally true; the choice depends on the specific use case and tools.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_36',
  'A company wants to ensure that their infrastructure deployments are always consistent, regardless of who runs the deployment or how many times it''s run. Which IaC principle is most relevant here?',
  'mcq',
  '["Idempotency","Modularity","Encapsulation","Polymorphism"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the principle of idempotency. It ensures that applying the same configuration multiple times will always result in the same desired infrastructure state, making deployments consistent and repeatable. Option B (incorrect) refers to breaking down configurations into reusable components. Option C (incorrect) refers to bundling data and methods that operate on the data within one unit. Option D (incorrect) refers to the ability of an object to take on many forms, typically in object-oriented programming. These are software engineering concepts not directly related to the ''same result every time'' aspect of infrastructure deployment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_37',
  'Terraform is considered a declarative tool because:',
  'mcq',
  '["You describe the desired end state of your infrastructure, and Terraform determines the actions.","You provide a step-by-step script for Terraform to execute in a specific order.","It requires explicit API calls to be defined in the configuration files.","It only supports creating new resources, not modifying existing ones."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) defines why Terraform is declarative. Users state ''what'' they want their infrastructure to be, and Terraform''s engine calculates the necessary operations to achieve that state. Option B (incorrect) describes an imperative approach, which is not how Terraform works (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001). Option C (incorrect) is false; Terraform abstracts API calls through providers. Option D (incorrect) is false; Terraform can create, modify, and destroy resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_38',
  'A DevOps team is adopting Terraform to manage its cloud infrastructure. They want to ensure that applying the same Terraform configuration multiple times will always result in the same infrastructure state, without unintended side effects or changes if the desired state is already met. What characteristic of Terraform''s approach does this describe?',
  'mcq',
  '["Scalability, allowing the infrastructure to grow with demand.","Elasticity, enabling resources to shrink when not needed.","Idempotency, ensuring consistent results regardless of how many times a configuration is applied.","Portability, allowing the code to run on any operating system."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) describes idempotency. Terraform is designed to be idempotent, meaning that applying the same configuration repeatedly will bring the infrastructure to the desired state, and if it''s already in that state, no further changes will be made. This ensures predictability and reliability in infrastructure deployments. Option A (incorrect) and Option B (incorrect) relate to the dynamic scaling capabilities of cloud resources, which can be managed by IaC but are not a characteristic of Terraform''s configuration application itself. Option D (incorrect) refers to software portability, which is not directly related to how Terraform applies infrastructure configurations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_39',
  'When using Terraform, an engineer defines the desired end state of the infrastructure in configuration files. Terraform then determines the necessary actions to achieve that state. This approach contrasts with specifying a sequence of commands to execute. What term best describes Terraform''s operational model?',
  'mcq',
  '["Imperative, as it specifies the exact steps to perform.","Declarative, as it describes the desired state rather than the steps to reach it.","Procedural, requiring a strict order of operations.","Transactional, ensuring all changes are committed or rolled back together."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes Terraform''s declarative model. In a declarative approach, you define ''what'' you want the infrastructure to look like (the desired end state), and the tool (Terraform) figures out ''how'' to get there. Option A (incorrect) describes an imperative approach, where you explicitly list the sequence of commands or steps to achieve a result. This is a common misconception (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001) as Terraform code might look like programming, but its underlying model is declarative. Option C (incorrect) is similar to imperative, emphasizing a strict order. Option D (incorrect) describes a property of database operations, not the general approach to infrastructure provisioning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_40',
  'What is the fundamental concept behind Infrastructure as Code (IaC)?',
  'mcq',
  '["Managing infrastructure through manual console operations and graphical user interfaces.","Defining and managing infrastructure resources using machine-readable definition files.","Automating application deployment using scripting languages without managing underlying infrastructure.","Utilizing physical servers in a data center and configuring them directly."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) provides the fundamental definition of Infrastructure as Code. IaC is the practice of managing and provisioning infrastructure through code, using machine-readable definition files (like Terraform''s HCL) rather than manual processes or interactive configuration tools. Option A (incorrect) describes traditional manual infrastructure management, which IaC aims to replace. Option C (incorrect) describes application deployment automation, which is related but distinct from managing the infrastructure itself. Option D (incorrect) refers to on-premises physical infrastructure, which can also be managed by IaC but doesn''t define the concept of IaC itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_41',
  'Which statement best defines Infrastructure as Code (IaC)?',
  'mcq',
  '["A methodology for manually configuring cloud resources through a web console.","The practice of managing and provisioning computing infrastructure through code rather than manual processes.","A programming paradigm that emphasizes mutable infrastructure components.","A set of security policies applied to cloud environments to prevent unauthorized access."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) provides the most accurate and concise definition of Infrastructure as Code. It emphasizes managing and provisioning infrastructure through code, moving away from manual, error-prone processes. Option A (incorrect) describes the opposite of IaC, which aims to automate and codify infrastructure. Option C (incorrect) is incorrect; IaC often promotes immutable infrastructure. Option D (incorrect) describes security policies, which are a separate but related concern that can be managed by IaC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_42',
  'What is the primary role of Terraform within the Infrastructure as Code (IaC) paradigm?',
  'mcq',
  '["To provide a graphical user interface for cloud resource management.","To enable the definition, provisioning, and management of infrastructure using a declarative configuration language.","To offer a platform for developing custom cloud-native applications.","To perform real-time monitoring and logging of infrastructure events."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes Terraform''s primary role. Terraform is an IaC tool that allows users to define, provision, and manage infrastructure across various cloud providers and on-premises environments using its declarative HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL). Option A (incorrect) is false; Terraform is primarily CLI-driven. Option C (incorrect) describes application development platforms, not an IaC tool. Option D (incorrect) describes monitoring services, which are distinct from infrastructure provisioning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_43',
  'A team uses Terraform to manage their cloud infrastructure. They frequently apply the same configuration to update their development environment. What characteristic ensures that these repeated applications only make necessary changes and do not cause unintended side effects?',
  'mcq',
  '["The imperative nature of Terraform''s execution.","The transactional rollback capabilities of cloud providers.","The idempotency of Terraform operations.","The built-in auto-scaling features of the cloud platform."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) refers to idempotency. Terraform''s idempotent nature ensures that applying the same configuration multiple times will consistently bring the infrastructure to the desired state, making only the necessary changes and leaving the infrastructure untouched if it already matches the desired state. Option A (incorrect) is the opposite of Terraform''s declarative nature (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001). Option B (incorrect) refers to database-like operations, not the core characteristic of an IaC tool''s application logic. Option D (incorrect) relates to dynamic resource management, which is separate from how configuration changes are applied.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_44',
  'A team is developing a new microservices application and needs to provision a complex network of virtual private clouds, subnets, and security groups. They want to avoid the time-consuming and error-prone process of clicking through a cloud provider''s console. Which aspect of Terraform''s declarative approach makes this efficient?',
  'mcq',
  '["It requires explicit, step-by-step commands for each resource creation.","It allows defining the entire desired infrastructure state in a single configuration, and Terraform handles the provisioning logic.","It automatically generates application code for the microservices.","It provides real-time monitoring of network traffic and resource utilization."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) highlights the efficiency of Terraform''s declarative approach. Instead of specifying individual steps, engineers define the complete desired state of the complex network. Terraform then intelligently determines the order and actions required to achieve that state, automating the provisioning logic. Option A (incorrect) describes an imperative approach, which is contrary to Terraform''s declarative nature (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001). Option C (incorrect) is outside the scope of an IaC tool. Option D (incorrect) describes monitoring, not provisioning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_45',
  'A development team often uses shell scripts to provision resources, which involves a sequence of API calls. They are considering migrating to Terraform. What is a key conceptual difference between their current scripting approach and Terraform''s approach to infrastructure provisioning?',
  'mcq',
  '["Shell scripts are declarative, while Terraform is imperative.","Shell scripts are imperative, focusing on ''how'' to achieve a state, while Terraform is declarative, focusing on ''what'' the desired state is.","Both shell scripts and Terraform are purely imperative, differing only in syntax.","Both shell scripts and Terraform are purely declarative, differing only in supported cloud providers."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) highlights the key conceptual difference. Shell scripts are typically imperative, specifying a sequence of commands (''how'' to do something). Terraform, conversely, is declarative; you define the desired end state of your infrastructure (''what'' you want), and Terraform determines the necessary actions to achieve it. Option A (incorrect) reverses the definitions. Option C (incorrect) is wrong because Terraform is declarative. Option D (incorrect) is wrong because shell scripts are imperative.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_46',
  'A DevOps team uses Terraform to manage their cloud infrastructure. When they run `terraform plan`, they observe that Terraform calculates the exact sequence of API calls required to transition the infrastructure from its current state to the desired state defined in the configuration files. This behavior demonstrates that Terraform operates on a model where:',
  'mcq',
  '["The user explicitly defines the sequence of operations Terraform should execute.","Terraform is a declarative tool that determines the necessary actions to achieve the desired state.","Terraform acts as an imperative scripting engine, executing commands in a predefined order.","The cloud provider''s API directly dictates the order of resource creation and modification."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes Terraform''s declarative model. Users define the desired end state, and Terraform''s planning engine (the `terraform plan` command) intelligently calculates the optimal sequence of actions (API calls) required to reach that state. Option A (incorrect) is false; users define the desired state, not the explicit sequence of operations. Option C (incorrect) is a common misconception (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001); while Terraform executes actions, its core model is declarative, not imperative. Option D (incorrect) is partially true in that APIs are used, but Terraform orchestrates the calls based on its declarative understanding.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_47',
  'A company is setting up a new CI/CD pipeline for its cloud infrastructure. They want to ensure that every time the pipeline runs, the infrastructure is deployed or updated reliably, without manual intervention, and that repeated runs don''t cause unintended changes if the infrastructure is already in the desired state. Which IaC principle is crucial for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["The imperative nature of infrastructure provisioning.","The ability to manually approve each step of the deployment.","Idempotency, ensuring consistent results regardless of repeated application.","The use of proprietary scripting languages for automation."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is idempotency. This principle ensures that applying the same configuration multiple times will always yield the same result, making it safe and reliable for automated CI/CD pipelines. If the infrastructure is already in the desired state, Terraform will detect no changes and perform no actions. Option A (incorrect) is contrary to Terraform''s declarative nature (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001). Option B (incorrect) introduces manual intervention, which CI/CD aims to minimize. Option D (incorrect) is not a core principle of IaC and can lead to vendor lock-in.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'hard'
);

