INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_17',
  'How do you explicitly assign a resource to a specific aliased provider in Terraform?',
  'mcq',
  '["By adding a `provider_alias` argument to the resource block.","By using the `provider` argument within the resource block, referencing the alias.","By placing the resource block inside the aliased `provider` block.","By setting an environment variable before running `terraform apply`."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is how you assign a resource to an aliased provider. Within a `resource` block, you use the `provider` argument, specifying the provider type and its alias (e.g., `provider = aws.us_east_1`). Option A is incorrect syntax. Option C is incorrect; resource blocks are top-level or within modules, not nested inside provider blocks. Option D is for provider configuration, not for assigning individual resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_18',
  'What is the benefit of using provider aliasing when deploying resources to multiple AWS accounts?',
  'mcq',
  '["It allows Terraform to automatically detect the correct account based on resource names.","It enables a single Terraform configuration to manage resources across different accounts without duplication.","It encrypts the provider credentials for each account automatically.","It reduces the number of resources that need to be defined in the configuration."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the benefit. Provider aliasing allows you to define multiple instances of the AWS provider, each configured with credentials for a different AWS account. This enables a single Terraform configuration to manage resources across these distinct accounts, reducing configuration duplication and simplifying management. Option A is incorrect; Terraform does not automatically detect accounts. Option C is incorrect; encryption is typically handled by other means. Option D is incorrect; it doesn''t reduce resource definitions, but rather consolidates the management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_19',
  'What happens if you define two `provider "aws"` blocks with the exact same configuration and no aliases?',
  'mcq',
  '["Terraform will use the first defined provider block and ignore the second.","Terraform will merge their configurations into a single provider.","Terraform will throw an error because provider configurations must be unique or aliased.","Terraform will automatically assign an alias to the second provider block."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is what happens. Terraform requires provider configurations to be unique. If you define multiple `provider` blocks for the same provider type without unique `alias` arguments, Terraform will report an error, as it cannot determine which configuration to use. Option A and B are incorrect; Terraform doesn''t silently ignore or merge. Option D is incorrect; Terraform does not automatically assign aliases.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_20',
  'A Terraform configuration uses the AWS provider and needs to interact with a specific AWS S3 endpoint that is not the default. Where would this custom endpoint be configured?',
  'mcq',
  '["In the `resource \"aws_s3_bucket\"` block.","In the `provider \"aws\"` block.","As an input variable to the root module.","In the `terraform` block''s `backend` configuration."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is where a custom endpoint for a service like S3 would be configured. The `provider "aws"` block allows you to specify service-specific endpoints (e.g., `s3_endpoint = "https://s3.custom.endpoint"`) to override the default. Option A is incorrect; resource blocks define the resource itself, not provider-level configuration. Option C might pass the value, but the actual configuration happens in the provider block. Option D is for state backend configuration, not service endpoints.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_21',
  'Which scenario most clearly demonstrates the need for provider aliasing?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploying a single EC2 instance to a single AWS region.","Managing resources in both AWS and Google Cloud Platform within one configuration.","Deploying an application stack to two distinct AWS accounts (e.g., dev and prod) from a single configuration.","Using different versions of the same provider in a single configuration."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) most clearly demonstrates the need for provider aliasing. When you need to interact with the same cloud provider (e.g., AWS) but with different credentials or configurations (like different accounts or regions), aliasing allows you to define multiple instances of that provider within a single Terraform configuration. Option A is a single provider use case. Option B involves different provider types, which don''t require aliasing for each other. Option D is handled by version constraints in `required_providers`, not aliasing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_22',
  'What is the primary reason for using multiple provider configurations in a single Terraform workspace?',
  'mcq',
  '["To manage resources across different cloud providers simultaneously.","To reduce the overall cost of infrastructure deployment.","To simplify the process of writing complex HCL code.","To enable automatic rollback of failed deployments."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) states the primary reason: to manage resources across different cloud providers simultaneously (e.g., AWS and Azure) or to interact with different instances of the same provider (e.g., multiple AWS accounts or regions). Option B is incorrect; using multiple providers doesn''t inherently reduce cost. Option C is incorrect; it can add complexity, not simplify. Option D is incorrect; automatic rollback is a deployment strategy, not a direct feature of multi-provider configurations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_23',
  'How do you configure multiple instances of the same provider (e.g., AWS in two different regions) within a single Terraform configuration?',
  'mcq',
  '["By creating separate Terraform configuration files for each region.","By using provider aliases in the `provider` block.","By defining multiple `resource` blocks for the same provider type.","By specifying the region directly in each resource block."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the way to configure multiple instances of the same provider. You use provider aliases in the `provider` block, like `provider "aws" { alias = "us-east-1" }` and `provider "aws" { alias = "eu-west-1" }`. Resources can then specify which aliased provider to use. Option A is incorrect; while possible, it defeats the purpose of managing them in a single configuration. Option C is incorrect; multiple `resource` blocks don''t configure the provider itself. Option D is incorrect; while some resources allow region specification, it''s not the standard way to configure multiple provider instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_24',
  'A team needs to deploy resources into two distinct AWS accounts from a single Terraform configuration. What is the most effective way to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Create two separate Terraform state files, one for each account.","Use two `provider \"aws\"` blocks with different `alias` names and respective authentication configurations.","Define all resources in a single `provider \"aws\"` block and let Terraform handle the account switching.","Manually switch AWS CLI profiles between `terraform plan` and `terraform apply`."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most effective way. By defining two `provider "aws"` blocks with distinct `alias` names (e.g., `aws.dev` and `aws.prod`) and configuring each with different authentication credentials (e.g., via `profile` or `assume_role`), you can manage resources in separate AWS accounts from a single configuration. Option A is incorrect; while possible, it doesn''t allow managing both accounts from a *single* configuration. Option C is incorrect; a single provider block cannot manage two distinct accounts simultaneously without aliases. Option D is incorrect; manual switching is error-prone and not scalable.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_25',
  'What happens if you define multiple `provider` blocks for the same provider type without using aliases?',
  'mcq',
  '["Terraform will automatically merge their configurations into a single provider instance.","Terraform will use the last defined `provider` block and ignore previous ones.","Terraform will raise an error, requiring you to use aliases for multiple instances.","Terraform will create separate provider instances but only allow one to be used per resource."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) states that Terraform will raise an error. If you define multiple `provider` blocks for the same provider type (e.g., `provider "aws" {}` twice) without specifying aliases, Terraform will detect a duplicate configuration and require you to use aliases to differentiate them. Option A is incorrect; merging is not automatic. Option B is incorrect; it''s an error, not an override. Option D is incorrect; it won''t create separate instances without aliases.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_26',
  'When is it appropriate to use the default provider configuration (without an alias) alongside aliased provider configurations?',
  'mcq',
  '["Never; all provider configurations must have an alias.","When most resources should use the default configuration, and only a few need a specific aliased configuration.","Only when managing resources in a single cloud region.","When you want to prevent any resources from being managed by the default provider."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the appropriate scenario. It''s common practice to define a default provider configuration (without an alias) for the majority of your resources, and then use aliased configurations only for the exceptions that need different settings (e.g., a different region or account). Resources that don''t explicitly specify a `provider` argument will use the default. Option A is incorrect; a default provider is often useful. Option C is incorrect; aliasing is useful for multiple regions/accounts. Option D is incorrect; the default provider is for resources that *should* be managed by it.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_27',
  'What information is typically configured within a `provider` block when setting up multiple providers or aliased providers?',
  'mcq',
  '["Resource names and their desired properties.","Input variables and output values for the configuration.","Authentication credentials, region, and other provider-specific settings.","The remote backend configuration for Terraform state."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) lists the typical information configured in a `provider` block. This includes authentication credentials (e.g., access keys, profiles), the target region, and other provider-specific settings that define how Terraform interacts with that particular instance of the cloud or service. Option A is incorrect; resource properties are in `resource` blocks. Option B is incorrect; variables and outputs are in `variable` and `output` blocks. Option D is incorrect; the remote backend is configured in the `terraform` block.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_28',
  'A company wants to manage resources in both AWS and Azure using a single Terraform configuration. How should they declare the providers?',
  'mcq',
  '["By defining a single `provider` block and specifying both AWS and Azure credentials within it.","By defining separate `provider \"aws\" {}` and `provider \"azurerm\" {}` blocks.","By using a single `resource` block that supports both AWS and Azure resources.","By creating separate configuration files for AWS and Azure and linking them."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the correct approach. To manage resources across different cloud providers like AWS and Azure, you define separate `provider` blocks for each, e.g., `provider "aws" {}` and `provider "azurerm" {}`. Terraform will then use the appropriate provider for each resource type. Option A is incorrect; a single provider block cannot handle multiple cloud providers. Option C is incorrect; there isn''t a single `resource` block that spans different providers. Option D is incorrect; while possible, the question implies a single configuration for both.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_29',
  'Consider a scenario where you need to create an S3 bucket in `us-east-1` and an EC2 instance in `eu-west-1` using a single Terraform configuration. How would you configure the AWS provider?',
  'mcq',
  '["Define one `provider \"aws\" {}` block with `region = \"us-east-1\"` and specify `region = \"eu-west-1\"` in the EC2 resource.","Define two `provider \"aws\"` blocks, one with `region = \"us-east-1\"` and another with `alias = \"europe\"` and `region = \"eu-west-1\"`.","Use a single `provider \"aws\" {}` block and let Terraform infer the region from the resource type.","Create separate Terraform workspaces for each region and switch between them."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the appropriate way to configure the AWS provider for this scenario. You would define a default `provider "aws" {}` block for `us-east-1` (or vice-versa) and then an aliased `provider "aws" { alias = "europe" region = "eu-west-1" }` block. The S3 bucket would implicitly use the default provider, and the EC2 instance would explicitly reference `provider = aws.europe`. Option A is incorrect; specifying region in the resource block is not how providers are configured for different regions. Option C is incorrect; Terraform does not infer regions from resource types. Option D is incorrect; the goal is a single configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_30',
  'What is the significance of the `default` provider configuration when using aliases?',
  'mcq',
  '["It is automatically used for all resources that do not explicitly specify a `provider` argument.","It is ignored if any aliased providers are defined in the configuration.","It serves as a template for all other aliased provider configurations.","It is only used for `terraform init` operations and not for resource management."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) states the significance of the `default` provider configuration. Any resource block that does not explicitly specify a `provider = "alias_name"` argument will automatically use the default provider configuration. This is useful for setting up a primary region or account. Option B is incorrect; it''s not ignored. Option C is incorrect; it''s not a template. Option D is incorrect; it''s used for all resource management operations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_31',
  'What is the correct syntax for defining an aliased AWS provider in the `provider` block?',
  'mcq',
  '["`provider \"aws\" { name = \"secondary\" region = \"us-west-2\" }`","`provider \"aws\" { alias = \"secondary\" region = \"us-west-2\" }`","`provider \"aws:secondary\" { region = \"us-west-2\" }`","`provider \"aws\" { instance = \"secondary\" region = \"us-west-2\" }`"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) shows the correct syntax for defining an aliased provider using the `alias` argument within the `provider` block. This creates a distinct instance of the AWS provider named ''secondary'' configured for `us-west-2`. Option A uses `name`, which is not the correct argument for aliasing. Option C uses an incorrect syntax for the provider block name. Option D uses `instance`, which is not a valid argument for provider aliasing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_70_m_70_3_32',
  'If a resource block does not specify a `provider` argument, which provider configuration will it use?',
  'mcq',
  '["It will use the first aliased provider defined in the configuration.","It will use the default provider configuration (the one without an `alias` argument).","Terraform will prompt the user to select a provider during `terraform plan`.","The resource creation will fail, requiring an explicit provider specification."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) states that if a resource block does not specify a `provider` argument, it will implicitly use the default provider configuration for that provider type (the one defined without an `alias` argument). Option A is incorrect; it doesn''t default to the first aliased provider. Option C is incorrect; Terraform does not prompt for provider selection. Option D is incorrect; it will only fail if no default provider is defined or if there are conflicting default definitions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain70_16',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_70_3',
  'medium'
);

