VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_4_11',
  'A DevOps team is managing a complex AWS infrastructure using Terraform. After making several changes to their `.tf` files, they want to understand exactly what modifications Terraform proposes to the live infrastructure before applying them. They need a detailed, human-readable summary that clearly outlines resource additions, updates, and deletions without making any actual changes. Which command should the team execute to achieve this, ensuring they can review the impact of their changes?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform apply -dry-run","terraform validate","terraform plan","terraform refresh"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) `terraform plan` is the command specifically designed to generate an execution plan, showing exactly what Terraform will do to the infrastructure based on the current configuration and state. It provides a detailed diff of proposed changes (additions, modifications, destructions) without actually performing them, allowing for review and approval. Option A (`terraform apply -dry-run`) is incorrect because `terraform apply` does not have a `-dry-run` flag; the `plan` command serves this purpose. Option B (`terraform validate`) checks the syntax and internal consistency of the configuration files but does not interact with the remote state or propose infrastructure changes. Option D (`terraform refresh`) updates the state file to reflect the current real-world infrastructure, but it does not show a diff of proposed changes from the configuration and is not intended for reviewing future modifications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_4_12',
  'A company is migrating a legacy application to AWS using Terraform. During development, a specific Amazon EC2 instance resource defined in their Terraform configuration has become unresponsive and needs to be completely replaced with a new instance, even if no configuration changes have been made to its resource block. The team wants to force Terraform to destroy the existing instance and create a new one during the next `terraform apply`. Which flag should be used with `terraform plan` or `terraform apply` to achieve this controlled replacement?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform apply -recreate=aws_instance.example","terraform apply -force-new=aws_instance.example","terraform apply -replace=aws_instance.example","terraform apply -destroy-and-create=aws_instance.example"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) `terraform apply -replace=aws_instance.example` is the correct way to force Terraform to replace a specific resource. This flag tells Terraform to taint the specified resource, marking it for destruction and recreation even if its configuration has not changed. The replacement will then be executed during the `apply` operation. Option A (`-recreate`), Option B (`-force-new`), and Option D (`-destroy-and-create`) are not valid flags for `terraform apply` or `terraform plan`. The `-replace` flag is the standard and supported method for this operation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_4_13',
  'A platform team manages shared infrastructure components like VPCs and security groups using Terraform. They need to update a specific security group rule without affecting any other resources in their configuration. To ensure only the intended security group is modified, they want to generate an execution plan that is scoped exclusively to that single resource. Which flag should be used with `terraform plan` to achieve this targeted planning?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform plan -only=aws_security_group.web_sg","terraform plan -resource=aws_security_group.web_sg","terraform plan -target=aws_security_group.web_sg","terraform plan -focus=aws_security_group.web_sg"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) `terraform plan -target=aws_security_group.web_sg` is the correct flag to generate an execution plan for a specific resource. The `-target` option limits the scope of the plan (and subsequent `apply` or `destroy`) to only the specified resource(s), ignoring all others in the configuration. This is useful for making isolated changes or troubleshooting. Options A (`-only`), B (`-resource`), and D (`-focus`) are not valid flags for `terraform plan`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_4_14',
  'A DevOps team is managing a critical production environment with Terraform. They have made a small, non-disruptive change to a tag on an Amazon S3 bucket. Before applying this change, they want to save the proposed execution plan to a file. This file will then be reviewed by a senior engineer and later used to apply the exact, approved changes, ensuring no unexpected modifications occur between planning and application. Which `terraform plan` option enables saving the plan to a file?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform plan -save=s3_tag_update.tfplan","terraform plan -out=s3_tag_update.tfplan","terraform plan -export=s3_tag_update.tfplan","terraform plan -file=s3_tag_update.tfplan"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) `terraform plan -out=s3_tag_update.tfplan` is the correct way to save the generated execution plan to a specified file. This plan file can then be passed to `terraform apply` (e.g., `terraform apply s3_tag_update.tfplan`) to ensure that only the exact changes previewed in the plan are applied. Options A (`-save`), C (`-export`), and D (`-file`) are not valid flags for `terraform plan`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_4_15',
  'A senior engineer is reviewing a `terraform plan` output for a critical production change. The plan shows that an existing AWS EC2 instance will be replaced, which is an unexpected and potentially disruptive action. The engineer needs to understand why Terraform believes this resource needs to be replaced instead of updated in-place. Which aspect of the `terraform plan` output would provide the most detailed information about the specific attributes causing the replacement?',
  'mcq',
  '["The ''Resource will be destroyed and recreated'' message, indicating a change in a non-updatable attribute.","The ''No changes. Infrastructure is up-to-date.'' message, which would be incorrect if a replacement is planned.","The ''Plan: X to add, Y to change, Z to destroy'' summary, providing a high-level overview.","The ''Refresh complete'' message, confirming the state file is synchronized with the real infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) When `terraform plan` indicates that a resource will be destroyed and recreated, it will explicitly state this. The detailed diff output for that resource will then show which specific attributes have changed and are marked with `(forces new resource)` or similar indicators, explaining why an in-place update is not possible and a replacement is necessary. Option B (incorrect) would indicate no changes, which contradicts the scenario. Option C (incorrect) provides a high-level summary but lacks the specific attribute-level detail needed to understand *why* a replacement is occurring. Option D (incorrect) confirms state synchronization but doesn''t explain the planned changes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_4_16',
  'A critical security patch requires an immediate update to an AWS Lambda function''s runtime version. The Terraform configuration has been updated to reflect the new runtime. The DevOps team needs to generate an execution plan to verify this specific change before applying it. They also want to ensure that the plan output is concise and easy to read, focusing only on the Lambda function. Which command would best achieve this, showing the proposed change to the runtime?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform plan -target=aws_lambda_function.my_function","terraform apply -dry-run -target=aws_lambda_function.my_function","terraform refresh -target=aws_lambda_function.my_function","terraform validate -target=aws_lambda_function.my_function"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) `terraform plan -target=aws_lambda_function.my_function` is the correct command to generate an execution plan specifically for a single resource. This will show the proposed changes (in this case, the runtime version update) only for the targeted Lambda function, making the output concise and focused. Option B (`terraform apply -dry-run`) is incorrect because `terraform apply` does not have a `-dry-run` flag. Option C (`terraform refresh -target=aws_lambda_function.my_function`) updates the state file for the targeted resource but does not show proposed changes from the configuration. Option D (`terraform validate -target=aws_lambda_function.my_function`) is incorrect because `terraform validate` checks configuration syntax and consistency, not proposed infrastructure changes, and the `-target` flag is not applicable to `validate`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_4_17',
  'What is the primary purpose of the `terraform plan` command?',
  'mcq',
  '["To create an execution plan that shows what actions Terraform will take to achieve the desired state.","To apply changes to the infrastructure based on the current configuration.","To initialize the working directory and download necessary provider plugins.","To validate the syntax and internal consistency of the Terraform configuration files."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (To create an execution plan that shows what actions Terraform will take to achieve the desired state) is the correct answer. `terraform plan` is used to preview the changes that Terraform will make to your infrastructure without actually executing them. This allows for review and verification. Option B (To apply changes to the infrastructure based on the current configuration) describes `terraform apply`. Option C (To initialize the working directory and download necessary provider plugins) describes `terraform init`. Option D (To validate the syntax and internal consistency of the Terraform configuration files) describes `terraform validate`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_4_18',
  'When `terraform plan` is executed, what type of output does it primarily provide?',
  'mcq',
  '["A diff-like summary of proposed resource creations, updates, and deletions.","A list of all resources currently managed by Terraform in the state file.","A detailed log of all API calls made to the cloud provider.","A graphical representation of the infrastructure dependencies."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (A diff-like summary of proposed resource creations, updates, and deletions) is the primary output of `terraform plan`. It shows a human-readable summary of the changes Terraform intends to make, indicating which resources will be added (+), changed (~), or destroyed (-). Option B (A list of all resources currently managed by Terraform in the state file) is provided by `terraform state list`. Option C (A detailed log of all API calls made to the cloud provider) is not the standard output of `terraform plan`, though verbose logging can be enabled. Option D (A graphical representation of the infrastructure dependencies) can be generated using `terraform graph`, not `terraform plan`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_4',
  'medium'
);

