INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_9',
  'What does the `terraform validate` command check for?',
  'mcq',
  '["Connectivity to cloud providers.","Availability of resources in the cloud.","Syntax and internal consistency of the configuration.","Cost implications of the planned infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is what `terraform validate` checks for. It verifies that the configuration files have correct syntax and are internally consistent, meaning all references and types are correctly defined. It does not perform any checks that require connecting to a cloud provider. Option A and B require provider interaction. Option D is typically part of `terraform plan` output or external tools.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  114,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_10',
  'What is the primary purpose of the `terraform validate` command?',
  'mcq',
  '["To check for syntax errors and internal consistency in configuration files.","To verify network connectivity to the configured cloud provider.","To compare the desired state with the actual infrastructure.","To apply changes to the infrastructure after approval."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the primary purpose of `terraform validate`. It performs static analysis of the configuration files to ensure they are syntactically correct and internally consistent, without requiring any connection to a remote backend or cloud provider. Option B and C involve interaction with the actual infrastructure or state. Option D describes `terraform apply`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  115,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_11',
  'Which command is used to ensure the Terraform configuration is syntactically valid and internally consistent before generating a plan or applying changes?',
  'mcq',
  '["`terraform fmt`","`terraform refresh`","`terraform validate`","`terraform plan`"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is `terraform validate`. This command performs a static analysis of the configuration to check for syntax errors and internal consistency without interacting with the actual infrastructure or state. Option A, `terraform fmt`, formats the code. Option B, `terraform refresh`, updates the state. Option D, `terraform plan`, generates an execution plan.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  116,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_12',
  'Which command checks the syntax and internal consistency of Terraform configuration files without connecting to any remote services?',
  'mcq',
  '["`terraform init`","`terraform plan`","`terraform validate`","`terraform refresh`"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is `terraform validate`. This command performs a static analysis of the configuration to ensure it is syntactically correct and internally consistent, without needing to interact with cloud providers or a remote state backend. Option A initializes. Option B plans changes. Option D refreshes state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  117,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_13',
  'A company''s CI/CD pipeline for Terraform deployments is failing during the `terraform plan` stage. The error messages indicate issues with resource arguments and block structures within the `.tf` files. The pipeline is designed to catch these errors early, before attempting to interact with the cloud provider. Which command, executed as an early step in the pipeline, would efficiently identify these configuration errors without requiring AWS credentials or network connectivity to AWS?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform apply -dry-run","terraform refresh","terraform validate","terraform fmt -check"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) `terraform validate` is the command designed to check the syntax and internal consistency of Terraform configuration files. It verifies that the configuration is syntactically valid and semantically consistent, identifying issues like incorrect resource arguments or block structures, all without needing to connect to a cloud provider or access remote state. Option A (`terraform apply -dry-run`) is incorrect as `terraform apply` does not have a `-dry-run` flag, and even if it did, it would require provider credentials. Option B (`terraform refresh`) interacts with the cloud provider to update the state file, which is not the goal here and requires credentials. Option D (`terraform fmt -check`) only verifies formatting compliance, not the semantic correctness of the configuration itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  118,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_14',
  'A Terraform configuration contains several modules and complex expressions. A developer wants to quickly check if the configuration is syntactically correct and adheres to Terraform''s language rules before running `terraform plan`. They are particularly concerned about typos in resource arguments or incorrect variable references that might cause runtime errors. Which command provides this static analysis without attempting to connect to any cloud provider?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform console","terraform refresh","terraform validate","terraform graph"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) `terraform validate` performs a static analysis of the configuration files, checking for syntax errors, incorrect variable references, and other internal consistency issues. It does this without requiring any interaction with the remote state or cloud providers, making it ideal for early error detection. Option A (`terraform console`) is for interactive expression evaluation. Option B (`terraform refresh`) interacts with the cloud provider to update the state file. Option D (`terraform graph`) generates a visual representation of the resource dependencies, but does not validate the configuration''s correctness.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  119,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_15',
  'A team is developing a complex Terraform configuration with several nested modules. They want to ensure that all variables passed between modules are correctly defined and that there are no type mismatches or missing required arguments. They need a command that performs a deep check of the entire configuration''s structure and references, without actually connecting to AWS. Which command is best suited for this comprehensive static analysis?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform plan -validate-only","terraform graph","terraform console","terraform validate"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) `terraform validate` performs a comprehensive check of the entire configuration, including nested modules and variable definitions. It verifies syntax, argument types, and internal consistency, ensuring that the configuration is semantically valid. This process does not require AWS credentials or network connectivity to AWS. Option A (`terraform plan -validate-only`) is incorrect; `terraform plan` does not have a `-validate-only` flag, and it would attempt to connect to AWS. Option B (`terraform graph`) visualizes resource dependencies but doesn''t validate configuration correctness. Option C (`terraform console`) is for interactive expression evaluation, not full configuration validation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  120,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_16',
  'A team is developing a complex Terraform configuration that uses several local modules. They want to ensure that all module calls correctly pass required variables and that no module is missing a `source` definition. They need a command that performs a static analysis of the entire configuration, including all modules, to catch these types of errors before any deployment. Which command should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform graph","terraform console","terraform validate","terraform plan -module-check"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) `terraform validate` is designed to perform a static analysis of the entire Terraform configuration, including local modules. It checks for syntax errors, correct variable usage, and ensures that all required arguments are provided, without connecting to a cloud provider. Option A (`terraform graph`) visualizes dependencies but doesn''t validate correctness. Option B (`terraform console`) is for interactive expression evaluation. Option D (`terraform plan -module-check`) is not a valid flag for `terraform plan`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  121,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_17',
  'What is the primary purpose of the `terraform validate` command?',
  'mcq',
  '["To check the syntax and internal consistency of Terraform configuration files.","To verify connectivity to the cloud provider and authenticate credentials.","To generate an execution plan showing proposed infrastructure changes.","To apply changes to the infrastructure based on the current configuration."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (To check the syntax and internal consistency of Terraform configuration files) is the correct answer. `terraform validate` is used to ensure that the configuration files are syntactically correct and internally consistent, checking for things like undeclared variables or invalid arguments, without interacting with the cloud provider. Option B (To verify connectivity to the cloud provider and authenticate credentials) is incorrect; `validate` does not typically connect to the provider. Option C (To generate an execution plan showing proposed infrastructure changes) describes `terraform plan`. Option D (To apply changes to the infrastructure based on the current configuration) describes `terraform apply`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  122,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_18',
  'Which of the following does `terraform validate` explicitly NOT check?',
  'mcq',
  '["Whether the configured AWS credentials are valid and have sufficient permissions.","Syntax errors in the `.tf` files.","Type mismatches for variables.","References to undeclared variables or resources."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Whether the configured AWS credentials are valid and have sufficient permissions) is correct. `terraform validate` performs static analysis of the configuration files. It does not attempt to connect to any cloud provider or validate credentials/permissions, as that would require live API calls. Option B (Syntax errors in the `.tf` files), Option C (Type mismatches for variables), and Option D (References to undeclared variables or resources) are all types of checks that `terraform validate` performs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  123,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_19',
  'A developer runs `terraform validate` after making changes to a configuration. The command returns an error message. What does this indicate?',
  'mcq',
  '["There are syntax errors or internal consistency issues in the configuration files.","The Terraform working directory has not been initialized correctly.","The cloud provider''s API is currently unreachable or experiencing issues.","The current Terraform state file is out of sync with the actual infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (There are syntax errors or internal consistency issues in the configuration files) is correct. A failure from `terraform validate` specifically means that Terraform found problems within the configuration itself, such as incorrect syntax, invalid arguments, or references to non-existent resources or variables. Option B (The Terraform working directory has not been initialized correctly) would typically lead to an error from `init` or subsequent commands, but `validate` can often run even without a full `init` for basic syntax checks. Option C (The cloud provider''s API is currently unreachable or experiencing issues) is incorrect, as `validate` does not connect to the provider. Option D (The current Terraform state file is out of sync with the actual infrastructure) is a concern addressed by `terraform plan` or `terraform refresh`, not `validate`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  124,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_20',
  'When is `terraform validate` typically run in the development lifecycle?',
  'mcq',
  '["Early and frequently, after making any changes to the configuration files.","Only immediately before running `terraform apply` in a production environment.","After `terraform apply` to confirm that the changes were successfully provisioned.","As the very first command in a new working directory, before `terraform init`."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Early and frequently, after making any changes to the configuration files) is correct. `terraform validate` is a fast, lightweight command that should be run often during development to catch configuration errors early, before attempting to generate a plan or apply changes. Option B (Only immediately before running `terraform apply` in a production environment) is too late; it should be run much earlier. Option C (After `terraform apply` to confirm that the changes were successfully provisioned) is incorrect; `validate` checks configuration, not deployed infrastructure. Option D (As the very first command in a new working directory, before `terraform init`) is incorrect; `terraform init` is always the first command.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  125,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_21',
  'A CI/CD pipeline includes a step for `terraform validate`. What is the primary benefit of this step?',
  'mcq',
  '["To catch configuration errors quickly without incurring cloud provider costs or making API calls.","To ensure that all necessary provider plugins and modules are downloaded.","To generate a detailed report of all planned infrastructure changes for auditing.","To automatically apply any necessary formatting changes to the Terraform code."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (To catch configuration errors quickly without incurring cloud provider costs or making API calls) is the primary benefit. `terraform validate` performs static analysis, meaning it can check for syntax and consistency errors without needing to connect to the cloud provider. This makes it a fast and cost-effective check in a CI/CD pipeline. Option B (To ensure that all necessary provider plugins and modules are downloaded) is the role of `terraform init`. Option C (To generate a detailed report of all planned infrastructure changes for auditing) is the role of `terraform plan`. Option D (To automatically apply any necessary formatting changes to the Terraform code) is the role of `terraform fmt`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  126,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_22',
  'What is the key difference between `terraform validate` and `terraform plan`?',
  'mcq',
  '["`validate` checks configuration syntax without connecting to providers, while `plan` connects to providers to propose infrastructure changes.","`validate` applies changes to infrastructure, while `plan` only previews them.","`validate` downloads provider plugins, while `plan` only uses already downloaded plugins.","`validate` is used for destroying resources, while `plan` is used for creating them."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (`validate` checks configuration syntax without connecting to providers, while `plan` connects to providers to propose infrastructure changes) is the correct distinction. `terraform validate` performs static analysis of the configuration locally. `terraform plan`, however, interacts with the configured providers to refresh the state and determine the actual changes needed in the cloud. Option B (`validate` applies changes to infrastructure, while `plan` only previews them) is incorrect; neither `validate` nor `plan` apply changes. Option C (`validate` downloads provider plugins, while `plan` only uses already downloaded plugins) is incorrect; `terraform init` downloads plugins. Option D (`validate` is used for destroying resources, while `plan` is used for creating them) is incorrect; `destroy` is for destruction, and `plan` previews all types of changes (create, update, destroy).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  127,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_3_23',
  'If `terraform validate` reports no errors, what does this guarantee about the configuration?',
  'mcq',
  '["The configuration is syntactically correct and internally consistent, but not necessarily deployable.","The configuration will apply successfully without any errors or warnings.","All resources defined in the configuration can be successfully provisioned in the cloud.","The configured AWS credentials have sufficient permissions for all operations."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (The configuration is syntactically correct and internally consistent, but not necessarily deployable) is correct. `terraform validate` only checks the configuration''s syntax and internal consistency. It does not guarantee that the configuration is semantically correct, that credentials are valid, or that the cloud provider will accept the proposed changes. These checks occur during `terraform plan` and `terraform apply`. Option B (The configuration will apply successfully without any errors or warnings) is incorrect; `validate` doesn''t guarantee successful application. Option C (All resources defined in the configuration can be successfully provisioned in the cloud) is incorrect; this requires live API calls. Option D (The configured AWS credentials have sufficient permissions for all operations) is incorrect; `validate` does not check permissions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  128,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_3',
  'medium'
);

