INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_44',
  'Which command in the Terraform workflow is responsible for provisioning or updating infrastructure resources according to the configuration?',
  'mcq',
  '["`terraform validate`","`terraform plan`","`terraform apply`","`terraform refresh`"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (`terraform apply`) is the command responsible for provisioning or updating infrastructure resources. It takes the execution plan (either generated on the fly or from a saved plan file) and makes the necessary changes to the real-world infrastructure. Option A (`terraform validate`) checks configuration syntax. Option B (`terraform plan`) shows what changes would occur. Option D (`terraform refresh`) updates the state file to match real infrastructure but doesn''t apply configuration changes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  162,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_45',
  'What is the purpose of the `terraform destroy` command in the Terraform workflow?',
  'mcq',
  '["To remove specific resources from the state file without affecting real infrastructure.","To delete all infrastructure resources managed by the current Terraform configuration.","To revert the last `terraform apply` operation.","To mark resources for replacement during the next `terraform apply`."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (To delete all infrastructure resources managed by the current Terraform configuration) is the primary purpose of `terraform destroy`. It generates a plan to destroy all resources defined in the configuration and then executes it upon confirmation. Option A is done by `terraform state rm`. Option C is incorrect; Terraform does not have a direct ''rollback'' command. Option D is done by `terraform apply -replace`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  163,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_46',
  'A company has several existing cloud resources that were manually created. They now want to bring these resources under Terraform management. Which command is designed for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["`terraform import`","`terraform attach`","`terraform sync`","`terraform adopt`"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (`terraform import`) is the command specifically designed to bring existing infrastructure resources under Terraform management. It reads the real-world state of a resource and adds it to the Terraform state file, allowing subsequent `terraform plan` and `apply` operations to manage it. Options B, C, and D are not standard Terraform commands for this purpose.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  164,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_47',
  'A Terraform configuration''s state file has drifted from the actual infrastructure due to manual changes made outside of Terraform. Which command can be used to reconcile the state file with the real-world infrastructure without applying any configuration changes?',
  'mcq',
  '["`terraform apply -refresh-only`","`terraform validate`","`terraform plan -destroy`","`terraform state push`"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (`terraform apply -refresh-only`) is the correct command to reconcile the Terraform state file with the real-world infrastructure without making any changes based on the configuration. It updates the state file to reflect the current attributes of the resources in the cloud. `terraform refresh` (which `apply -refresh-only` is equivalent to in modern Terraform versions for this specific task) is also an option, but `apply -refresh-only` is more explicit for state reconciliation. Option B (`terraform validate`) only checks configuration syntax. Option C (`terraform plan -destroy`) plans to destroy resources. Option D (`terraform state push`) is for manually pushing a local state to a remote backend.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  165,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_48',
  'A junior engineer attempts to fix an inconsistency in the Terraform state by directly opening and editing the `terraform.tfstate` file with a text editor. Why is this action considered an anti-pattern and highly discouraged?',
  'mcq',
  '["It is a valid method for minor state corrections.","Direct edits can easily corrupt the state file, leading to unpredictable behavior or data loss.","The `terraform.tfstate` file is encrypted and cannot be edited manually.","Terraform automatically reverts any manual changes upon the next command execution."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Direct edits can easily corrupt the state file, leading to unpredictable behavior or data loss) is correct. Manually editing the `terraform.tfstate` file is highly dangerous because it''s easy to introduce syntax errors, break internal consistency, or cause mismatches between the state and real infrastructure, which can lead to resource duplication, deletion, or other critical issues. Terraform provides `terraform state` subcommands (like `mv`, `rm`, `pull`, `push`) for safe state manipulation. Option A is incorrect; it''s never a valid method for state correction. Option C is incorrect; while state can contain sensitive data, it''s not always encrypted by default and can be viewed/edited. Option D is incorrect; Terraform will not automatically revert manual changes, but rather use the corrupted state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  166,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_49',
  'What is the primary purpose of Terraform workspaces?',
  'mcq',
  '["To manage multiple separate Terraform configurations within a single directory.","To allow multiple developers to concurrently modify the same state file.","To provide separate state files for different environments (e.g., dev, staging, prod) using the same configuration.","To define reusable blocks of Terraform code for different projects."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (To provide separate state files for different environments (e.g., dev, staging, prod) using the same configuration) is the primary purpose of Terraform workspaces. Workspaces allow you to manage multiple distinct instances of the same configuration, each with its own state file, within a single working directory. Option A is incorrect; workspaces don''t manage separate configurations, but separate instances of the *same* configuration. Option B is incorrect; state locking prevents concurrent modifications. Option D describes Terraform modules.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  167,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_50',
  'What is the correct sequence of the standard Terraform workflow steps?',
  'mcq',
  '["init, plan, apply, destroy","plan, init, apply, destroy","init, apply, plan, destroy","validate, init, plan, apply"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (init, plan, apply, destroy) represents the correct and standard sequence of the Terraform workflow. First, `init` initializes the working directory. Then, `plan` creates an execution plan. Next, `apply` executes the plan to provision infrastructure. Finally, `destroy` can be used to tear down the managed infrastructure. Option B, C, and D present incorrect or incomplete sequences.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  168,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_51',
  'In the Terraform workflow, what is the purpose of the `terraform init` command?',
  'mcq',
  '["To prepare the working directory for use by downloading necessary plugins and setting up the backend.","To show the changes that Terraform plans to make to the infrastructure.","To apply the proposed changes to the infrastructure.","To remove all resources managed by the current configuration."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (To prepare the working directory for use by downloading necessary plugins and setting up the backend) correctly describes the purpose of `terraform init`. It''s the first command run in a new Terraform working directory. Option B (To show the changes that Terraform plans to make to the infrastructure) describes `terraform plan`. Option C (To apply the proposed changes to the infrastructure) describes `terraform apply`. Option D (To remove all resources managed by the current configuration) describes `terraform destroy`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  169,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_52',
  'Which command in the Terraform workflow allows you to preview the changes Terraform will make to your infrastructure before they are applied?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform plan","terraform apply","terraform validate","terraform show"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (terraform plan) is the command used to create an execution plan, which shows exactly what actions Terraform will take (create, update, or destroy resources) to reach the desired state defined in the configuration. This allows for review before applying changes. Option B (terraform apply) executes the changes. Option C (terraform validate) checks configuration syntax and consistency. Option D (terraform show) displays the current state or a saved plan, but doesn''t generate a new plan of proposed changes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  170,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_53',
  'A team has finished developing and testing their infrastructure. They now want to remove all resources managed by their Terraform configuration. Which command should they use as the final step in the lifecycle?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform destroy","terraform clean","terraform delete","terraform remove"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (terraform destroy) is the correct command to deprovision all infrastructure resources managed by the current Terraform configuration. This is typically the final step when an environment is no longer needed. Options B, C, and D are not valid Terraform commands for this purpose.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  171,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_54',
  'What is the primary benefit of following the `init`, `plan`, `apply` workflow?',
  'mcq',
  '["It ensures predictable and safe infrastructure changes by allowing review before execution.","It automatically rolls back any failed infrastructure deployments to a previous stable state.","It optimizes resource utilization and reduces cloud costs by identifying unused resources.","It provides real-time monitoring and alerting for infrastructure health and performance."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (It ensures predictable and safe infrastructure changes by allowing review before execution) is the primary benefit. The `plan` step allows users to review exactly what changes Terraform intends to make, preventing unexpected or accidental modifications. This predictability is central to Terraform''s value proposition. Option B (It automatically rolls back any failed infrastructure deployments to a previous stable state) is incorrect; Terraform does not have an automatic rollback feature. Option C (It optimizes resource utilization and reduces cloud costs by identifying unused resources) is incorrect; while Terraform helps manage resources, it doesn''t inherently optimize or identify unused resources. Option D (It provides real-time monitoring and alerting for infrastructure health and performance) describes monitoring services, not the core Terraform workflow.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  172,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_55',
  'A user attempts to run `terraform apply` immediately after cloning a repository containing Terraform configuration files, without running any other commands. What is the most likely outcome?',
  'mcq',
  '["The command will fail because the working directory has not been initialized.","Terraform will automatically initialize the directory and then apply the changes.","The command will succeed, but no changes will be made to the infrastructure.","Terraform will prompt the user to run `terraform init` before proceeding with the apply."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (The command will fail because the working directory has not been initialized) is the most likely outcome. `terraform apply` requires the working directory to be initialized (`terraform init`) to download providers, set up backends, and prepare the environment. Without `init`, Terraform cannot understand the configuration or interact with the cloud provider. Option B (Terraform will automatically initialize the directory and then apply the changes) is incorrect; Terraform does not implicitly run `init` when `apply` is called. Option C (The command will succeed, but no changes will be made to the infrastructure) is incorrect; it will fail. Option D (Terraform will prompt the user to run `terraform init` before proceeding with the apply) is partially correct in that it will usually provide an error message indicating `init` is needed, but the command itself will still fail.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  173,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_56',
  'Which component of the Terraform workflow is responsible for maintaining the mapping between real-world infrastructure and your configuration?',
  'mcq',
  '["The Terraform state file.","The provider plugins.","The main configuration file (`.tf`).","The `.terraform` directory."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (The Terraform state file) is correct. The state file is a critical component of the Terraform workflow, as it records the mapping between the resources defined in your configuration and the real-world infrastructure objects that Terraform manages. It tracks metadata, resource IDs, and attributes. Option B (The provider plugins) are responsible for interacting with the cloud provider''s APIs, but they don''t maintain the mapping themselves. Option C (The main configuration file (`.tf`)) defines the desired state, not the current managed state. Option D (The `.terraform` directory) contains downloaded plugins and modules, but the state file within it (or in a backend) is what maintains the mapping.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  174,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'medium'
);

