INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_7',
  'A Terraform configuration defines an `aws_vpc` resource and an `aws_subnet` resource. The `aws_subnet` resource requires the `vpc_id` from the `aws_vpc` resource. Which of the following is the correct way to reference the `id` attribute of an `aws_vpc` resource named `main` within the `aws_subnet` resource?',
  'mcq',
  '["vpc_id = var.main_vpc_id","vpc_id = data.aws_vpc.main.id","vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id","vpc_id = local.vpc_main_id"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) `aws_vpc.main.id` is the correct syntax to reference the `id` attribute of an `aws_vpc` resource named `main`. The general format for referencing a resource attribute is `resource_type.resource_name.attribute_name`.

Option A (incorrect) `var.main_vpc_id` would be used if the VPC ID were passed in as an input variable. While valid for external IDs, it''s not how you reference an ID of a resource *defined within the same configuration*.

Option B (incorrect) `data.aws_vpc.main.id` would be used if you were referencing an *existing* VPC that Terraform does not manage, using a `data` block. This is for sourcing information, not for referencing a resource defined in the current configuration.

Option D (incorrect) `local.vpc_main_id` would be used if the VPC ID was assigned to a local value. While a local could be set to `aws_vpc.main.id`, the most direct way to reference the attribute is `aws_vpc.main.id` itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_8',
  'A Terraform configuration defines an `aws_instance` resource and an `aws_security_group` resource. The `aws_instance` needs to be associated with the `aws_security_group`. Which attribute of the `aws_security_group` resource should the `aws_instance` resource reference to establish this association and create an implicit dependency?',
  'mcq',
  '["The `name` attribute of the `aws_security_group`.","The `id` attribute of the `aws_security_group`.","The `arn` attribute of the `aws_security_group`.","The `description` attribute of the `aws_security_group`."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the `id` attribute. When associating an `aws_instance` with an `aws_security_group`, the `vpc_security_group_ids` argument of the `aws_instance` resource expects a list of security group IDs. Referencing `aws_security_group.example.id` establishes an implicit dependency, ensuring the security group is created before the instance attempts to associate with it.

Option A (incorrect) `name` attribute is used for identification but not typically for direct association in resource arguments that expect IDs or ARNs. While you can filter by name, direct association uses the ID.

Option C (incorrect) `arn` attribute is the Amazon Resource Name. While ARNs are used for many AWS resources, security groups are primarily referenced by their ID in EC2 instance configurations.

Option D (incorrect) `description` attribute is merely descriptive text and holds no functional value for resource association.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_9',
  'A Terraform configuration creates an AWS S3 bucket and an AWS S3 bucket object. The S3 object''s content is a local file, and its `bucket` argument must specify the name of the S3 bucket created by Terraform. Which attribute of the `aws_s3_bucket` resource should be referenced by the `aws_s3_bucket_object` resource to correctly specify the target bucket?',
  'mcq',
  '["The `id` attribute of the `aws_s3_bucket` resource.","The `arn` attribute of the `aws_s3_bucket` resource.","The `bucket_domain_name` attribute of the `aws_s3_bucket` resource.","The `bucket_regional_domain_name` attribute of the `aws_s3_bucket` resource."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the `id` attribute. For `aws_s3_bucket` resources, the `id` attribute typically holds the bucket''s name. The `aws_s3_bucket_object` resource''s `bucket` argument expects the name of the S3 bucket. Therefore, referencing `aws_s3_bucket.example.id` is the correct way to specify the bucket name and establish an implicit dependency.

Option B (incorrect) `arn` attribute provides the Amazon Resource Name of the bucket. While important for IAM policies, the `bucket` argument of `aws_s3_bucket_object` specifically requires the bucket name, not the ARN.

Option C (incorrect) `bucket_domain_name` provides the global domain name for the bucket (e.g., `example.s3.amazonaws.com`). This is not the bucket name itself.

Option D (incorrect) `bucket_regional_domain_name` provides the regional domain name for the bucket (e.g., `example.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com`). This is also not the bucket name itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_10',
  'A Terraform configuration defines an `aws_vpc` and an `aws_internet_gateway`. The `aws_internet_gateway` needs to be attached to the `aws_vpc`. Which attribute of the `aws_vpc` resource should be referenced by the `aws_internet_gateway` resource to establish this attachment and ensure the VPC exists first?',
  'mcq',
  '["The `cidr_block` attribute of the `aws_vpc`.","The `id` attribute of the `aws_vpc`.","The `instance_tenancy` attribute of the `aws_vpc`.","The `enable_dns_hostnames` attribute of the `aws_vpc`."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the `id` attribute. The `aws_internet_gateway` resource has a `vpc_id` argument that expects the ID of the VPC to which it should be attached. Referencing `aws_vpc.example.id` correctly establishes this link and creates an implicit dependency, ensuring the VPC is provisioned before the internet gateway attempts to attach to it.

Option A (incorrect) `cidr_block` specifies the IP address range for the VPC. It''s a configuration detail of the VPC, not an identifier for attachment.

Option C (incorrect) `instance_tenancy` specifies whether instances launched in the VPC run on shared or dedicated hardware. This is irrelevant for attaching an internet gateway.

Option D (incorrect) `enable_dns_hostnames` controls whether instances in the VPC get public DNS hostnames. This is also irrelevant for attaching an internet gateway.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_11',
  'A Terraform configuration creates an AWS DynamoDB table and an AWS IAM policy that grants read/write access to this table. The IAM policy document needs to specify the ARN of the DynamoDB table. Which attribute of the `aws_dynamodb_table` resource should be referenced in the IAM policy document to correctly identify the table?',
  'mcq',
  '["The `name` attribute of the `aws_dynamodb_table`.","The `id` attribute of the `aws_dynamodb_table`.","The `arn` attribute of the `aws_dynamodb_table`.","The `stream_arn` attribute of the `aws_dynamodb_table`."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the `arn` attribute. AWS IAM policies require the Amazon Resource Name (ARN) to specify the target resource for permissions. Therefore, referencing `aws_dynamodb_table.example.arn` is the correct way to identify the DynamoDB table within an IAM policy document.

Option A (incorrect) `name` attribute is the name of the DynamoDB table. While useful for human readability, IAM policies typically use ARNs for resource identification.

Option B (incorrect) `id` attribute for `aws_dynamodb_table` is also its name. Similar to the `name` attribute, it''s not the ARN required for IAM policies.

Option D (incorrect) `stream_arn` attribute is the ARN of the DynamoDB Stream associated with the table, if one is enabled. This is distinct from the table''s own ARN and would grant permissions to the stream, not the table itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_12',
  'A Terraform configuration creates an AWS S3 bucket and an AWS S3 bucket public access block. The public access block needs to be associated with the S3 bucket. Which attribute of the `aws_s3_bucket` resource should the `aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block` resource reference to correctly identify the target bucket?',
  'mcq',
  '["The `bucket_domain_name` attribute of the `aws_s3_bucket`.","The `id` attribute of the `aws_s3_bucket`.","The `arn` attribute of the `aws_s3_bucket`.","The `region` attribute of the `aws_s3_bucket`."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the `id` attribute. For `aws_s3_bucket` resources, the `id` attribute typically holds the bucket''s name. The `aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block` resource''s `bucket` argument expects the name of the S3 bucket. Therefore, referencing `aws_s3_bucket.example.id` is the correct way to specify the bucket name and establish an implicit dependency.

Option A (incorrect) `bucket_domain_name` provides the global domain name for the bucket (e.g., `example.s3.amazonaws.com`). This is not the bucket name itself.

Option C (incorrect) `arn` attribute provides the Amazon Resource Name of the bucket. While important for IAM policies, the `bucket` argument of `aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block` specifically requires the bucket name, not the ARN.

Option D (incorrect) `region` attribute specifies the AWS region where the bucket resides. This is a property of the bucket, not its identifier for association.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_13',
  'A Terraform configuration defines an AWS SQS queue and an AWS Lambda function. The Lambda function needs to be granted permission to send messages to the SQS queue. The SQS queue resource block is named `aws_sqs_queue.my_queue`. How would the Lambda function''s IAM policy reference the ARN of this SQS queue to establish an implicit dependency?',
  'mcq',
  '["By using `aws_sqs_queue.my_queue.id` directly in the IAM policy.","By explicitly adding `depends_on = [aws_sqs_queue.my_queue]` to the Lambda function.","By referencing `aws_sqs_queue.my_queue.arn` within the IAM policy definition.","By defining a `local` variable for the SQS queue ARN and using that in the policy."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the standard way to reference an attribute of another resource. Terraform automatically infers an implicit dependency when `aws_sqs_queue.my_queue.arn` is used in the IAM policy. This ensures the SQS queue is created before the IAM policy that references its ARN. Option A (incorrect) `aws_sqs_queue.my_queue.id` typically refers to the queue name, not its ARN, and the ARN is usually required for IAM policies. Option B (incorrect) explicitly adding `depends_on` is generally not necessary when a direct attribute reference exists, as Terraform will infer the dependency. Option D (incorrect) defining a `local` variable for the SQS queue ARN and using that in the policy is a valid way to organize code, but the `local` variable itself still needs to reference `aws_sqs_queue.my_queue.arn`, which is where the implicit dependency is formed.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_14',
  'A Terraform configuration defines an AWS S3 bucket and an AWS CloudFront distribution. The CloudFront distribution needs to be configured to serve content from the S3 bucket. If the S3 bucket is destroyed, the CloudFront distribution should also be destroyed. What is the most natural way Terraform handles this relationship?',
  'mcq',
  '["An explicit `depends_on` meta-argument must be added to the CloudFront distribution, referencing the S3 bucket.","Terraform automatically infers a destruction dependency because the CloudFront distribution references the S3 bucket''s attributes.","A `lifecycle` block with `prevent_destroy = false` must be set on both resources.","A `data` block must be used to link the S3 bucket to the CloudFront distribution."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is how Terraform naturally handles this. When the CloudFront distribution resource references attributes of the S3 bucket resource (e.g., `aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket.bucket_regional_domain_name`), Terraform establishes an implicit dependency. This means Terraform understands that the S3 bucket must exist for the CloudFront distribution to function, and if the S3 bucket is destroyed, the CloudFront distribution that depends on it will also be destroyed (or updated to remove the dependency, depending on the specific configuration). Option A (incorrect) an explicit `depends_on` is generally not needed when an implicit dependency can be inferred through attribute references. Option C (incorrect) `prevent_destroy = false` is the default behavior and doesn''t explicitly create or manage destruction dependencies; `prevent_destroy = true` would prevent destruction. Option D (incorrect) a `data` block is for reading existing resources, not for managing the lifecycle or dependencies of resources being created by the current configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_15',
  'A Terraform configuration defines an AWS EC2 instance and an AWS Elastic IP (EIP). The EIP needs to be associated with the EC2 instance. The `aws_instance` resource is named `web_server`. How would you reference the ID of the EC2 instance from the EIP association resource to create an implicit dependency?',
  'mcq',
  '["`aws_instance.web_server.private_ip`","`aws_instance.web_server.id`","`aws_instance.web_server.arn`","`aws_instance.web_server.public_ip`"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) `aws_instance.web_server.id` is the correct attribute to reference the ID of the EC2 instance. When the `aws_eip_association` resource uses this ID, Terraform implicitly understands that the `web_server` instance must be created before the EIP can be associated with it. Option A (incorrect) `aws_instance.web_server.private_ip` refers to the private IP address, not the instance ID. Option C (incorrect) `aws_instance.web_server.arn` refers to the Amazon Resource Name, which is a different identifier. Option D (incorrect) `aws_instance.web_server.public_ip` refers to the public IP address, not the instance ID.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_16',
  'A Terraform configuration defines an AWS S3 bucket and an AWS IAM user. You want to ensure that the IAM user is created before any bucket policies are applied that reference the user''s ARN. How does Terraform typically handle this dependency?',
  'mcq',
  '["You must add `depends_on = [aws_iam_user.example]` to the `aws_s3_bucket_policy` resource.","Terraform automatically infers an implicit dependency when the IAM user''s ARN is referenced in the bucket policy.","You should use a `local-exec` provisioner to delay the bucket policy application.","The `lifecycle` block''s `create_before_destroy` argument handles this ordering."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is how Terraform typically handles this. When the `aws_s3_bucket_policy` resource''s JSON policy document references `aws_iam_user.example.arn`, Terraform automatically creates an implicit dependency. This ensures the `aws_iam_user` resource is created and its ARN is available before the `aws_s3_bucket_policy` attempts to use it. Option A (incorrect) explicitly adding `depends_on` is generally not needed when an implicit dependency can be inferred through attribute references. Option C (incorrect) using a `local-exec` provisioner is an anti-pattern for managing resource dependencies and introduces unnecessary complexity. Option D (incorrect) `create_before_destroy` is for managing resource replacement order, not for ensuring one resource exists before another is configured based on its attributes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_17',
  'A Terraform configuration defines an AWS VPC and an AWS Subnet within that VPC. The Subnet resource needs to reference the ID of the VPC. How does Terraform determine that the VPC must be created before the Subnet?',
  'mcq',
  '["By explicitly setting `depends_on` on the Subnet resource to the VPC resource.","By the alphabetical order of the resource names in the configuration file.","By the implicit dependency created when the Subnet resource references the VPC''s ID.","By using a `data` block to fetch the VPC ID before creating the Subnet."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is how Terraform handles this. When the `aws_subnet` resource references an attribute of the `aws_vpc` resource (e.g., `aws_vpc.main.id`), Terraform automatically infers an implicit dependency. This means Terraform understands that the VPC must be created and its ID available before the Subnet can be created. Option A (incorrect) explicitly setting `depends_on` is generally not needed when an implicit dependency can be inferred. Option B (incorrect) Terraform does not rely on the alphabetical order of resource names for dependency resolution. Option D (incorrect) using a `data` block would be for referencing an *existing* VPC not managed by the current configuration, not for establishing a dependency between resources being created within the same configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_18',
  'How does Terraform establish an implicit dependency between two resources?',
  'mcq',
  '["When one resource''s configuration directly references an attribute of another resource.","By explicitly using the `depends_on` meta-argument in one of the resource blocks.","By defining both resources within the same `.tf` configuration file.","By declaring both resources within the same Terraform module."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) describes how implicit dependencies are formed. Terraform automatically builds a dependency graph by analyzing resource attribute references. If resource A uses an attribute from resource B, resource B must be created before resource A. Option B describes an explicit dependency, not an implicit one. Options C and D are incorrect; the physical location of resource definitions (same file or module) does not automatically create dependencies; it''s the attribute references that matter.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_19',
  'What is the correct syntax to reference the ID of an `aws_vpc` resource named `main`?',
  'mcq',
  '["`aws_vpc.main.id`","`var.aws_vpc_main_id`","`resource.aws_vpc.main.id`","`data.aws_vpc.main.id`"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the standard and correct syntax for referencing an attribute of a managed resource. It follows the pattern `resource_type.resource_name.attribute_name`. Option B is incorrect; `var.` is used for input variables. Option C is incorrect; `resource.` is not used as a prefix for referencing resource attributes. Option D is incorrect; `data.` is used for data sources, which read existing infrastructure, not for attributes of resources managed by the current configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_20',
  'Which type of dependency is automatically managed by Terraform based on resource attribute references?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implicit dependency","Explicit dependency","Conditional dependency","External dependency"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the type of dependency automatically managed by Terraform. When one resource''s configuration uses an attribute from another resource, Terraform infers that the latter must be created before the former. Option B refers to dependencies explicitly defined using `depends_on`. Options C and D are not standard Terraform dependency types.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_21',
  'An `aws_subnet` resource needs to be created within an `aws_vpc` resource. How is the dependency between these two resources typically handled in Terraform?',
  'mcq',
  '["By referencing the `aws_vpc.example.id` attribute in the `vpc_id` argument of the `aws_subnet` resource.","By adding a `depends_on` meta-argument to the `aws_subnet` block, explicitly listing the `aws_vpc` resource.","By ensuring the `aws_vpc` block is defined physically before the `aws_subnet` block in the configuration file.","By using a `data` block to fetch the VPC ID after the `aws_vpc` resource has been created."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) describes the standard and most common way to establish an implicit dependency. When the `aws_subnet` resource''s `vpc_id` argument references `aws_vpc.example.id`, Terraform automatically understands that the VPC must exist before the subnet can be created. Option B is unnecessary and less idiomatic, as an implicit dependency already exists. Option C is incorrect; the physical order of blocks in a file does not determine dependency order. Option D is incorrect; a `data` block is for querying existing infrastructure, not for attributes of resources being managed in the current configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_72_m_72_2_22',
  'A team is deploying an AWS S3 bucket and an IAM policy that grants access to this bucket. The IAM policy needs to include the exact ARN of the S3 bucket. Which method should be used to ensure the IAM policy correctly references the S3 bucket''s ARN with the LEAST amount of manual effort?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use an implicit dependency by referencing `aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket.arn` directly within the IAM policy resource''s `policy` argument.","Use an explicit `depends_on` meta-argument on the IAM policy resource, listing the S3 bucket.","Manually hardcode the S3 bucket ARN into the IAM policy resource after the bucket has been created.","Create a `local` variable for the S3 bucket ARN and reference that in the IAM policy."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most efficient and idiomatic approach. By referencing `aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket.arn` directly in the IAM policy, Terraform automatically establishes an implicit dependency, ensuring the S3 bucket is created and its ARN is available before the IAM policy is evaluated. Option B is unnecessary because an implicit dependency already exists. Option C is a manual, error-prone process that defeats the purpose of Infrastructure as Code. Option D would still require referencing the S3 bucket''s ARN to define the local, so it doesn''t simplify the dependency management compared to direct reference.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain72_16',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_72_2',
  'medium'
);

