INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_77',
  'A team wants to improve collaboration and auditability for their infrastructure changes. They have decided to adopt Terraform and integrate it with their existing development practices. Order the steps that leverage version control and enable peer review for infrastructure changes.',
  'ordering',
  '["Merge the approved pull request to the main branch, triggering deployment.","Write or modify Terraform configuration files to define desired changes.","Submit a pull request for the Terraform configuration changes to a version control system.","Review the proposed changes in the pull request for correctness and adherence to standards."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'The process of collaborative infrastructure changes with auditability begins with the actual work. Option B (Write or modify Terraform configuration files to define desired changes) is the initial action. To enable peer review, Option C (Submit a pull request for the Terraform configuration changes to a version control system) is the next step. The review process then takes place, as described in Option D (Review the proposed changes in the pull request for correctness and adherence to standards). Once approved, the changes are integrated, which is Option A (Merge the approved pull request to the main branch, triggering deployment).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_78',
  'A DevOps engineer is managing a production environment with Terraform. They need to update a resource''s tag without causing any other unintended changes to existing infrastructure. Order the steps to ensure only the desired change is applied, demonstrating idempotency.',
  'ordering',
  '["Run `terraform plan` to preview the proposed changes and confirm only the tag is modified.","Verify that only the tag change is listed in the plan output.","Modify the tag attribute in the Terraform configuration file.","Run `terraform apply` to execute the changes after plan approval."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'To safely apply a specific change while ensuring idempotency, the process begins with the modification. Option C (Modify the tag attribute in the Terraform configuration file) is the first step. To validate the change, Option A (Run `terraform plan` to preview the proposed changes and confirm only the tag is modified) is executed. Critical verification follows with Option B (Verify that only the tag change is listed in the plan output). Finally, the change is implemented by Option D (Run `terraform apply` to execute the changes after plan approval).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_79',
  'A developer is learning Terraform and needs to understand how it provisions infrastructure in a declarative manner. They are asked to outline the internal process Terraform follows. Order the internal steps Terraform takes to provision resources declaratively.',
  'ordering',
  '["Terraform compares the desired state defined in configuration to the actual infrastructure state.","Terraform applies the plan by making API calls to the cloud provider.","The Terraform configuration defines the desired end state of the infrastructure.","Terraform generates an execution plan based on the calculated differences."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'The declarative provisioning process in Terraform starts with the definition of the target state. Option C (The Terraform configuration defines the desired end state of the infrastructure) is the initial input. Terraform then performs a reconciliation, as in Option A (Terraform compares the desired state defined in configuration to the actual infrastructure state). Based on this comparison, it formulates a strategy, making Option D (Terraform generates an execution plan based on the calculated differences) the next step. Finally, Terraform executes this strategy, as described in Option B (Terraform applies the plan by making API calls to the cloud provider).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_80',
  'A team wants to minimize human error during infrastructure deployments. They are considering adopting Infrastructure as Code with Terraform. Order the actions that lead to a reduction in configuration errors using IaC.',
  'ordering',
  '["Automated validation checks are performed on the configuration syntax and logic.","Manual configuration steps are eliminated from the deployment process.","Infrastructure is defined in machine-readable configuration files (HCL).","The configuration is version-controlled and peer-reviewed by team members."]'::json,
  '{2,3,0,1}',
  'Reducing configuration errors starts with a structured definition. Option C (Infrastructure is defined in machine-readable configuration files (HCL)) provides a clear, auditable blueprint. To catch errors early, Option D (The configuration is version-controlled and peer-reviewed by team members) adds a human and historical check. Further automation for error detection comes from Option A (Automated validation checks are performed on the configuration syntax and logic). The ultimate reduction in human error is achieved by Option B (Manual configuration steps are eliminated from the deployment process).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_81',
  'A large enterprise is adopting Terraform to manage its hybrid cloud infrastructure, aiming for consistency and reduced manual errors. The development team, new to Infrastructure as Code, needs to understand the standard declarative workflow for provisioning and updating resources. Order the following steps to reflect the typical Terraform workflow for applying configuration changes.',
  'ordering',
  '["Review the proposed infrastructure changes and execution plan generated by Terraform.","Apply the planned changes to provision or update the infrastructure.","Write the desired infrastructure state in HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL) files.","Initialize the working directory, downloading necessary providers and modules."]'::json,
  '{2,3,0,1}',
  'The correct sequence for a typical Terraform workflow begins with defining the desired infrastructure state. Option C (Write the desired infrastructure state in HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL) files) is the initial step where infrastructure is defined declaratively. Option D (Initialize the working directory, downloading necessary providers and modules) follows, preparing the environment for Terraform operations. Next, Option A (Review the proposed infrastructure changes and execution plan generated by Terraform) allows for verification of what Terraform intends to do before any changes are made. Finally, Option B (Apply the planned changes to provision or update the infrastructure) executes the plan, making the actual infrastructure changes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_82',
  'Arrange the following stages in the typical lifecycle of managing infrastructure with Terraform, starting from initial configuration.',
  'ordering',
  '["Review the execution plan for proposed changes","Initialize the Terraform working directory","Apply the changes to provision or modify resources","Write the desired infrastructure state in HCL"]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'The typical Terraform workflow begins with writing the infrastructure definition in HCL files (Option D). Next, `terraform init` (Option B) initializes the working directory, downloading necessary providers. After initialization, `terraform plan` (Option A) is used to generate and review an execution plan, showing what Terraform will do. Finally, `terraform apply` (Option C) executes the plan to create, update, or delete resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_83',
  'Order the following statements to correctly define the concept of Infrastructure as Code (IaC).',
  'ordering',
  '["Managing infrastructure through machine-readable definition files","Treating infrastructure configuration like software code","Automating the provisioning and management of infrastructure","Eliminating manual configuration processes"]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Infrastructure as Code (IaC) fundamentally involves treating infrastructure configuration like software code (Option B). This means managing infrastructure through machine-readable definition files (Option A), typically in a declarative language. The ultimate goal is automating the provisioning and management of infrastructure (Option C), which in turn leads to eliminating manual configuration processes (Option D) that are prone to errors.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_84',
  'Order the following statements to illustrate how Terraform''s declarative approach differs from an imperative approach to infrastructure provisioning.',
  'ordering',
  '["Terraform determines the necessary actions to reach the desired state","The user specifies the desired end state of the infrastructure","An imperative tool requires explicit step-by-step instructions","The user directly issues commands to modify resources"]'::json,
  '{2,3,1,0}',
  'An imperative tool requires explicit step-by-step instructions (Option C) for provisioning. With such a tool, the user directly issues commands to modify resources (Option D) in a specific sequence. In contrast, Terraform''s declarative approach means the user specifies the desired end state of the infrastructure (Option B). Terraform then determines the necessary actions to reach the desired state (Option A) without the user having to define the exact sequence of API calls.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_85',
  'Order the steps a user would take to update an existing Terraform-managed infrastructure configuration.',
  'ordering',
  '["Run `terraform apply` to implement the changes","Modify the existing Terraform configuration files","Execute `terraform plan` to preview the changes","Ensure the working directory is initialized with `terraform init`"]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}',
  'To update existing Terraform-managed infrastructure, the first step is to modify the existing Terraform configuration files (Option B) to reflect the desired changes. Before applying, it''s crucial to ensure the working directory is initialized with `terraform init` (Option D) to fetch any new provider versions. Next, execute `terraform plan` (Option C) to preview the proposed changes and verify they align with expectations. Finally, run `terraform apply` (Option A) to implement these changes in the infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_86',
  'Order the following steps that demonstrate Terraform''s idempotency during an `apply` operation.',
  'ordering',
  '["Terraform detects no changes are needed for existing resources","The `terraform apply` command is executed multiple times","The infrastructure state remains consistent after each apply","Terraform compares the desired state with the actual state"]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'Terraform''s idempotency is demonstrated when the `terraform apply` command is executed multiple times (Option B) with the same configuration. Each time, Terraform compares the desired state (defined in HCL) with the actual state (recorded in the state file and observed in the cloud) (Option D). If no changes are required, Terraform detects no changes are needed for existing resources (Option A) and takes no action. Consequently, the infrastructure state remains consistent after each apply (Option C), yielding the same result every time.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_87',
  'Order the following concepts to explain the ''desired state'' approach in Infrastructure as Code.',
  'ordering',
  '["IaC tool compares desired state to current actual state","Infrastructure is defined in a declarative configuration file","IaC tool takes actions to reconcile any differences","The configuration specifies the target end-state"]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'The ''desired state'' approach begins with infrastructure being defined in a declarative configuration file (Option B). This configuration specifies the target end-state (Option D) of the infrastructure, rather than a sequence of steps. The IaC tool then compares this desired state to the current actual state (Option A) of the infrastructure. Based on this comparison, the IaC tool takes actions to reconcile any differences (Option C), bringing the actual state in line with the desired state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_88',
  'Order the following actions that occur when Terraform detects a drift between the desired state and the actual infrastructure state.',
  'ordering',
  '["Terraform generates a plan to reconcile the drift","The actual infrastructure state is manually changed outside Terraform","Terraform compares the current configuration with the remote state","The `terraform apply` command brings the infrastructure back to desired state"]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'Drift detection starts when the actual infrastructure state is manually changed outside Terraform (Option B). When `terraform plan` or `terraform apply` is run, Terraform compares the current configuration (desired state) with the remote state (actual state) (Option C). If differences are found, Terraform generates a plan to reconcile the drift (Option A), outlining the actions needed to align the actual state with the desired state. Finally, the `terraform apply` command (Option D) executes this plan, bringing the infrastructure back to its desired state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_89',
  'Order the following commands to correctly destroy all resources managed by a Terraform configuration.',
  'ordering',
  '["Confirm the destruction by typing ''yes''","Run `terraform destroy` to initiate the process","Review the plan of resources to be destroyed","Ensure the working directory is initialized with `terraform init`"]'::json,
  '{3,1,2,0}',
  'Before destroying resources, ensure the working directory is initialized with `terraform init` (Option D) to ensure Terraform has the necessary provider plugins and state information. Then, run `terraform destroy` (Option B) to initiate the destruction process. Terraform will then present a plan of resources to be destroyed (Option C) for review. Finally, confirm the destruction by typing ''yes'' (Option A) when prompted to proceed with deleting the resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_90',
  'Order the following actions to illustrate the concept of ''idempotency'' in the context of Terraform.',
  'ordering',
  '["Terraform detects no changes are needed to the infrastructure","A Terraform configuration is applied multiple times","The infrastructure reaches and maintains the desired state","Terraform compares the desired state with the current state"]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'Idempotency in Terraform means that if a Terraform configuration is applied multiple times (Option B), it will always yield the same result. This is achieved because Terraform compares the desired state (from the configuration) with the current state (from the state file and actual infrastructure) (Option D). If the states match, Terraform detects no changes are needed to the infrastructure (Option A). As a result, the infrastructure reaches and maintains the desired state (Option C) without unintended side effects from repeated applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_91',
  'Order the following statements to correctly describe the ''declarative'' nature of Terraform.',
  'ordering',
  '["Terraform computes the actions needed to achieve the target state","The configuration specifies the desired end-state of the infrastructure","Terraform compares the desired state with the current actual state","Users do not specify the sequence of API calls"]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}',
  'Terraform''s declarative nature means the configuration specifies the desired end-state of the infrastructure (Option B), rather than a series of steps. Consequently, users do not specify the sequence of API calls (Option D); Terraform handles that. Instead, Terraform compares the desired state with the current actual state (Option C) of the infrastructure. Based on this comparison, Terraform computes the actions needed to achieve the target state (Option A).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_92',
  'Order the following elements to correctly describe the purpose of the Terraform state file.',
  'ordering',
  '["Maps real-world infrastructure resources to configuration","Tracks metadata about managed resources","Records the current actual state of the infrastructure","Used by Terraform to plan and apply changes"]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}',
  'The Terraform state file primarily records the current actual state of the infrastructure (Option C) that Terraform manages. It tracks metadata about managed resources (Option B), such as their IDs, attributes, and dependencies. Crucially, it maps real-world infrastructure resources to configuration (Option A) resources defined in HCL. This state file is then used by Terraform to plan and apply changes (Option D), by comparing the desired state with the recorded actual state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_69_m_69_1_93',
  'Order the following actions that occur when Terraform performs a `plan` operation.',
  'ordering',
  '["Terraform compares the desired state with the current state","Terraform reads the HCL configuration files","Terraform fetches the current state of remote resources","Terraform generates an execution plan of proposed changes"]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'When Terraform performs a `plan` operation, it first reads the HCL configuration files (Option B) to understand the desired state. Next, it fetches the current state of remote resources (Option C) from the cloud provider. Terraform then compares the desired state (from HCL) with the current state (from remote resources and the state file) (Option A). Based on this comparison, Terraform generates an execution plan of proposed changes (Option D), outlining what it will do if `apply` is run.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain69_16',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_69_1',
  'medium'
);

