  '["To rewrite Terraform configuration files to a canonical format.","To ensure consistent code style across a development team.","To validate the syntax and internal consistency of configuration files.","To apply changes to the infrastructure based on the configuration.","To download and install necessary provider plugins for the configuration."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) states that `terraform fmt` rewrites configuration files to a canonical format. This command automatically adjusts indentation, spacing, and other stylistic elements. Option B (correct) indicates that it ensures consistent code style across a development team. By enforcing a standard format, `terraform fmt` helps maintain readability and reduces style-related conflicts in version control. Option C (incorrect) describes `terraform validate`. Option D (incorrect) describes `terraform apply`. Option E (incorrect) describes `terraform init`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_44',
  'Which aspects of Terraform configuration files does `terraform fmt` standardize? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Indentation and spacing.","Ordering of arguments within blocks.","Provider plugin versions.","Resource naming conventions.","Backend configuration settings."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) states that `terraform fmt` standardizes indentation and spacing. It applies a consistent style to whitespace within the HCL code. Option B (correct) indicates that it standardizes the ordering of arguments within blocks. `terraform fmt` reorders arguments to a canonical order, which helps reduce unnecessary diffs in version control. Option C (incorrect) `terraform fmt` does not manage provider plugin versions; this is handled by the `terraform init` command and version constraints in the configuration. Option D (incorrect) `terraform fmt` does not enforce resource naming conventions; this is a policy decision for the team. Option E (incorrect) `terraform fmt` does not modify backend configuration settings; it only formats the HCL code.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_45',
  'Which of the following are benefits of consistently using `terraform fmt` in a development workflow? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Reduces merge conflicts related to stylistic changes in version control.","Improves readability and maintainability of Terraform code.","Automatically resolves syntax errors in the configuration files.","Ensures that the infrastructure state file is always up-to-date.","Downloads and updates Terraform provider plugins."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) states that `terraform fmt` reduces merge conflicts. By enforcing a canonical format, it minimizes diffs caused by stylistic variations between developers. Option B (correct) indicates that it improves readability and maintainability. Consistent formatting makes the code easier to read, understand, and debug for all team members. Option C (incorrect) `terraform fmt` only addresses formatting; it does not fix syntax errors, which are checked by `terraform validate`. Option D (incorrect) `terraform fmt` has no interaction with the state file; `terraform apply` or `terraform refresh` update the state. Option E (incorrect) `terraform init` is responsible for downloading and updating provider plugins.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
