INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_55',
  'Which of the following are true statements about the behavior of the `terraform fmt` command? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It rewrites configuration files to a canonical format.","It can be run recursively on a directory containing multiple `.tf` files.","It validates the syntax of the configuration files.","It connects to the cloud provider to check resource definitions.","It requires `terraform init` to be run beforehand."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is the core function of `terraform fmt`; it standardizes the formatting of Terraform configuration files. Option B (correct) is also true; by default, `terraform fmt` operates recursively on the current directory and its subdirectories, processing all `.tf` files found. Option C (incorrect) is false; `terraform fmt` does not validate syntax; that is the role of `terraform validate`. Option D (incorrect) is false; `terraform fmt` is a local operation and does not interact with any cloud providers. Option E (incorrect) is false; `terraform fmt` can be run at any time, even before `terraform init`, as it only operates on local files.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_56',
  'A team wants to ensure that all Terraform configuration files adhere to a consistent style. Which `terraform fmt` flags are useful for checking compliance without making changes? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["`-check` to verify if files are already formatted correctly.","`-diff` to show differences without applying changes.","`-recursive` to process all files in subdirectories.","`-write=false` to prevent any modifications.","`-list=false` to suppress listing files."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is specifically designed for this purpose; `-check` will exit with a non-zero status if any files are not formatted correctly, making it ideal for CI/CD checks. Option B (correct) `-diff` will show a diff of what changes `terraform fmt` *would* make without actually writing those changes to disk, allowing for review. Option C (incorrect) `-recursive` is a useful flag, but it dictates the scope of files to check, not whether changes are written. It is often used *with* `-check` or `-diff`. Option D (incorrect) `-write=false` is not a valid flag for `terraform fmt`. The `-check` flag implicitly prevents writing. Option E (incorrect) `-list=false` is not a valid flag; `terraform fmt` lists files by default if changes are made or if `-check` or `-diff` is used.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_57',
  'Which of the following are common reasons for a `terraform fmt -check` command to fail in a CI/CD pipeline? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Incorrect indentation in a `.tf` file.","Misordered arguments within a resource block.","Syntax errors in the Terraform configuration.","Missing required input variables.","Provider plugins not being initialized."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) and Option B (correct) are direct formatting issues that `terraform fmt` is designed to detect and correct. If these are present, `terraform fmt -check` will fail. Option C (incorrect) syntax errors are detected by `terraform validate`, not `terraform fmt`. Option D (incorrect) missing input variables would cause `terraform plan` or `apply` to fail, but not `terraform fmt`. Option E (incorrect) provider plugins are managed by `terraform init` and are not relevant to the purely local formatting check performed by `terraform fmt`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_58',
  'Which of the following are benefits of using `terraform fmt` as part of a team''s version control strategy? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Reduces merge conflicts related to whitespace and formatting changes.","Improves code readability and maintainability for all team members.","Automatically resolves logical conflicts in the configuration.","Enforces security best practices in resource definitions.","Ensures all resources are tagged consistently."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a significant benefit; by standardizing formatting, `terraform fmt` minimizes spurious diffs and merge conflicts that arise from different developers using different formatting styles. Option B (correct) is also a key advantage; consistent formatting makes the code easier to read, understand, and maintain across the team. Option C (incorrect) `terraform fmt` only addresses style, not logical conflicts or errors in the configuration. Option D (incorrect) `terraform fmt` does not enforce security best practices; this would require static analysis tools or policy enforcement. Option E (incorrect) `terraform fmt` does not enforce tagging consistency; this would be handled by configuration standards or policy tools.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_59',
  'When `terraform fmt` is run, what is its primary objective regarding the Terraform configuration files? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["To ensure all files conform to a canonical style.","To reorder resource attributes consistently.","To validate the syntax and semantics of the HCL code.","To detect and fix provider-specific configuration errors.","To download and update provider plugins."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is the overarching goal of `terraform fmt`: to apply a standardized, canonical formatting style to all `.tf` and `.tfvars` files. Option B (correct) is a specific aspect of this canonical formatting; `terraform fmt` reorders attributes within blocks (e.g., `resource`, `variable`) to a consistent, alphabetical order. Option C (incorrect) is the role of `terraform validate`, not `terraform fmt`. Option D (incorrect) is false; `terraform fmt` does not interact with providers or detect configuration errors beyond formatting. Option E (incorrect) is the role of `terraform init`, not `terraform fmt`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_60',
  'Which of the following are benefits of using `terraform fmt` in a collaborative environment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Reduces cognitive load for developers by standardizing code appearance.","Streamlines code reviews by focusing on logic rather than style.","Automatically updates provider versions to the latest compatible ones.","Prevents accidental deletion of resources during development.","Enforces naming conventions for resources and variables."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a benefit because consistent formatting makes it easier for developers to read and understand code written by others, reducing the mental effort required. Option B (correct) is also true; by automating style enforcement, `terraform fmt` allows code reviewers to focus their attention on the architectural and logical correctness of the infrastructure code, rather than superficial formatting issues. Option C (incorrect) is handled by `terraform init -upgrade`, not `terraform fmt`. Option D (incorrect) `terraform fmt` does not prevent resource deletion; that is a function of `terraform plan` and `apply` review. Option E (incorrect) `terraform fmt` does not enforce naming conventions; this is a policy or linter concern.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_61',
  'What are the primary benefits of regularly running `terraform fmt` on your configuration files? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It validates the configuration syntax for errors.","It ensures a consistent code style across all Terraform files.","It applies infrastructure changes defined in the configuration.","It significantly improves the readability and maintainability of the code.","It downloads necessary provider plugins for the configuration."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (correct) is true because `terraform fmt` enforces a canonical, consistent style for all `.tf` and `.tfvars` files, which is crucial for team collaboration. Option D (correct) is true as consistent formatting makes the code easier to read, understand, and maintain, reducing cognitive load for developers. Option A is incorrect; `terraform validate` is used for syntax validation. Option C is incorrect; `terraform apply` is used to apply infrastructure changes. Option E is incorrect; `terraform init` is responsible for downloading provider plugins.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_62',
  'How does `terraform fmt` contribute to better collaboration in a team environment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It automatically resolves infrastructure drift between team members'' environments.","It reduces merge conflicts that arise from stylistic differences in code.","It enforces a shared and consistent coding standard across the team.","It manages remote state locking to prevent concurrent modifications.","It performs security checks on the Terraform configuration."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (correct) is true because by standardizing code style, `terraform fmt` eliminates trivial differences in whitespace or indentation that can cause unnecessary merge conflicts in version control. Option C (correct) is true as it ensures all team members adhere to the same formatting guidelines, leading to a unified and predictable codebase. Option A is incorrect; `terraform fmt` is for code style, not for resolving infrastructure drift. Option D is incorrect; remote state locking is handled by the backend configuration, not `terraform fmt`. Option E is incorrect; security checks are typically performed by other tools or manual review, not `terraform fmt`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_63',
  'When integrating `terraform fmt` into a CI/CD pipeline, what are its common uses? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["To execute infrastructure changes automatically.","To verify that code adheres to formatting standards before merging.","To automatically format code as part of a pre-commit hook or build step.","To generate execution plans for review.","To destroy resources after testing is complete."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (correct) is true because `terraform fmt -check` can be used in CI to ensure that all submitted code is properly formatted, failing the build if any files are not compliant. Option C (correct) is true as `terraform fmt` can be run as part of a pre-commit hook or a CI build step to automatically format the code, ensuring consistency without manual intervention. Option A is incorrect; `terraform apply` is used to execute infrastructure changes. Option D is incorrect; `terraform plan` generates execution plans. Option E is incorrect; `terraform destroy` is used to destroy resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
