INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_28',
  'A developer has just cloned a new Terraform project from a Git repository. Which of the following commands are essential to run first to prepare the local working directory for any Terraform operations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["`terraform init` to download providers and set up the backend.","`terraform validate` to check the configuration for syntax errors.","`terraform plan` to preview the infrastructure changes.","`terraform apply` to deploy the infrastructure.","`terraform fmt` to ensure consistent code formatting."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'When starting a new Terraform project from a repository, initialization and validation are critical first steps. Option A (correct) `terraform init` is mandatory. It downloads necessary provider plugins, initializes modules, and sets up the configured backend, making the working directory functional for Terraform commands. Option B (correct) `terraform validate` should be run early to check the configuration for syntax errors and internal consistency. This helps catch basic issues before attempting more complex operations like planning or applying. Option C (incorrect) `terraform plan` is used to preview changes after the configuration is ready, not as an initial setup step. Option D (incorrect) `terraform apply` deploys infrastructure and should only be run after initialization, validation, and planning. Option E (incorrect) `terraform fmt` is for code formatting and while good practice, it''s not strictly required to *prepare* the working directory for operations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  153,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_29',
  'A Terraform configuration uses a remote backend (e.g., Amazon S3) and several custom modules. Which of the following actions are performed by `terraform init`? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Downloading necessary provider plugins.","Configuring the remote state backend.","Validating the syntax of the configuration files.","Generating an execution plan.","Applying changes to the infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  '`terraform init` prepares the working directory. Option A (correct) `terraform init` downloads all necessary provider plugins (e.g., `aws` provider) that are declared in the configuration. Option B (correct) it configures the remote state backend (e.g., Amazon S3) as specified in the configuration, ensuring that state is stored and retrieved correctly. Option C (incorrect) validating the syntax of the configuration files is the role of `terraform validate`. Option D (incorrect) generating an execution plan is the role of `terraform plan`. Option E (incorrect) applying changes to the infrastructure is the role of `terraform apply`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  154,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_30',
  'When `terraform init` is executed, which of the following directories and files are typically created or updated in the working directory? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["The `.terraform/` directory containing provider plugins and modules.","A `terraform.tfstate` file if a local backend is used and no state exists.","A `.terraform.lock.hcl` file to record provider dependency selections.","A `terraform.plan` file with the execution plan.","A `terraform.tfvars` file for variable definitions."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  '`terraform init` sets up the working directory. Option A (correct) the `.terraform/` directory is created or updated to store downloaded provider plugins, modules, and other internal Terraform data. Option C (correct) a `.terraform.lock.hcl` file is created or updated to record the exact versions and checksums of provider dependencies, ensuring consistent provider selection across different runs and environments. Option B (incorrect) a `terraform.tfstate` file is created by `terraform apply` when resources are first provisioned, not by `terraform init`. `init` only configures the backend where state will be stored. Option D (incorrect) a `terraform.plan` file is created by `terraform plan -out=filename`, not by `terraform init`. Option E (incorrect) a `terraform.tfvars` file is a user-created file for defining variable values; it is not created by `terraform init`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  155,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_31',
  'Which of the following are valid reasons to run `terraform init` with the `-upgrade` flag? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["To upgrade provider plugins to their latest compatible versions.","To ensure that all modules are updated to their latest source versions.","To force a re-download of all providers and modules regardless of current versions.","To reconfigure the backend without prompting for confirmation.","To apply pending infrastructure changes."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'The `-upgrade` flag for `terraform init` is used for updating dependencies. Option A (correct) `terraform init -upgrade` instructs Terraform to upgrade provider plugins to the newest available versions that satisfy the version constraints in the configuration. Option B (correct) similarly, it ensures that all modules used in the configuration are updated to their latest source versions, respecting any specified version constraints. Option C (incorrect) while it upgrades, it doesn''t necessarily force a re-download *regardless* of current versions if they are already the latest compatible; it checks for newer compatible versions. Option D (incorrect) reconfiguring the backend is primarily handled by `-reconfigure`, not `-upgrade`. Option E (incorrect) applying pending infrastructure changes is the role of `terraform apply`, not `terraform init`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  156,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_32',
  'Which of the following actions are performed by the `terraform init` command? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It creates an execution plan for infrastructure changes.","It downloads necessary provider plugins.","It sets up the configured backend for state management.","It applies infrastructure changes to the cloud.","It validates the syntax of Terraform configuration files."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (correct) `terraform init` downloads and installs the provider plugins required by the configuration. Option C (correct) it configures the backend for storing the Terraform state file, such as Amazon S3 or HashiCorp Consul. Option A (incorrect) `terraform plan` creates an execution plan. Option D (incorrect) `terraform apply` applies infrastructure changes. Option E (incorrect) `terraform validate` validates the syntax.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  157,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_33',
  'Which of the following are valid reasons to run `terraform init` with the `-upgrade` flag? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["To force Terraform to download the latest available versions of providers and modules.","To reconfigure the backend without changing the existing state.","To upgrade the Terraform CLI version itself to the latest stable release.","To ensure that all local modules are up-to-date with their source.","To apply pending infrastructure changes to the cloud."]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (correct) `terraform init -upgrade` instructs Terraform to attempt to upgrade provider plugins and modules to their latest compatible versions. Option D (correct) this flag specifically ensures that all local modules are updated to their latest source definitions. Option B (incorrect) `-reconfigure` is used for reconfiguring the backend, not `-upgrade`. Option C (incorrect) `terraform init -upgrade` does not upgrade the Terraform CLI itself; that''s a separate installation process. Option E (incorrect) `terraform apply` is used to apply infrastructure changes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  158,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_34',
  'Which of the following directories and files are typically created or managed by `terraform init`? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["`.terraform/` directory containing provider plugins.","`terraform.tfstate` file for state management.","`.terraform.lock.hcl` for dependency locking.","`.git/` directory for version control.","`terraform.tfvars` for variable definitions."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) `terraform init` creates the `.terraform/` directory, which stores downloaded providers, modules, and other internal data. Option C (correct) `terraform init` also creates or updates the `.terraform.lock.hcl` file to record the exact provider versions and their checksums, ensuring consistent dependency resolution. Option B (incorrect) `terraform.tfstate` is created during the first `terraform apply`, not `terraform init`. Option D (incorrect) `.git/` is managed by Git, not Terraform. Option E (incorrect) `terraform.tfvars` is a user-created file for variable definitions, not created by `terraform init`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  159,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_35',
  'A team is setting up a new Terraform project that uses a custom module hosted in a private Git repository. Which `terraform init` flags or configurations are relevant for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["`-backend-config` to specify module source.","`-input=false` to prevent interactive prompts for module credentials.","`-get=false` to skip downloading modules.","`-from-module=SOURCE` to clone the module.","`-upgrade` to ensure the latest version of the module is fetched."]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Option B (correct) when using private modules, `terraform init` might prompt for credentials. Using `-input=false` ensures non-interactive execution, assuming credentials are provided via environment variables or other non-interactive means. Option E (correct) `-upgrade` ensures that `terraform init` attempts to fetch the latest available version of the module from its source, which is often desired when working with custom modules. Option A (incorrect) `-backend-config` is for configuring the state backend, not module sources. Option C (incorrect) `-get=false` would *prevent* downloading modules, which is the opposite of what''s needed. Option D (incorrect) `-from-module` is used to generate a new configuration from an existing module, not to fetch a module for use within the current configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  160,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'medium'
);

