INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_4_12',
  'A development team wants to ensure that their infrastructure deployments are stable and not affected by unexpected breaking changes in upstream modules. What is the most effective strategy to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Regularly update all modules to their latest versions to benefit from new features.","Remove the `version` argument from module blocks to always use the newest available version.","Pin module versions using specific version numbers or patch-level constraints.","Only use modules from local paths, avoiding the Terraform Registry entirely."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most effective strategy. Pinning module versions (e.g., `version = "1.2.3"` or `version = "~> 1.2.0"`) ensures that Terraform always uses a known, tested version, preventing unexpected breaking changes from new module releases. Option A is a valid practice for keeping up-to-date but should be done after testing, not as a primary stability measure. Option B (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (`ap-5.1-001`) that leads to unpredictable behavior and potential breaking changes. Option D is impractical for most projects, as it removes the benefits of public modules.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_4_13',
  'What happens if you omit the `version` argument for a module sourced from the Terraform Registry?',
  'mcq',
  '["Terraform will prompt you to manually enter a version during `terraform init`.","Terraform will automatically select the oldest available version of the module.","Terraform will use the version of the module that was most recently published.","Terraform will download the latest available version of the module, which may include breaking changes."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) describes the behavior. If no `version` argument is specified for a Terraform Registry module, `terraform init` will download the latest available version. This is an anti-pattern (`ap-5.1-001`) because it can introduce unexpected breaking changes or regressions into your configuration. Option A is incorrect; Terraform does not prompt for versions. Option B is incorrect; it selects the latest. Option C is essentially the same as D and highlights the risk.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_4_14',
  'How can you ensure that a Terraform module uses an exact, specific version and will not automatically update to any newer patch or minor releases?',
  'mcq',
  '["Set the `version` argument to a precise number, like `\"1.2.3\"`.","Use the `~>` operator, such as `version = \"~> 1.2.3\"`.","Specify `version = \">= 1.2.3\"` in the module block.","Remove the `version` argument to always use the default stable version."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the way to pin to an exact version. By providing a precise version number (e.g., `"1.2.3"`), Terraform will only use that specific version. Option B (`~>`) allows patch-level updates (or minor updates if only major is specified). Option C (`>=`) allows any newer version, including major, minor, and patch. Option D is an anti-pattern that leads to using the latest, potentially breaking, version.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_4_15',
  'When sourcing a module from the Terraform Registry, where is the `version` argument typically specified?',
  'mcq',
  '["In the module''s `variables.tf` file.","In the `required_providers` block of the root module.","Within the `module` block itself, alongside the `source` argument.","In the `terraform.tfvars` file for the root module."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is where the `version` argument is specified. It is an argument within the `module` block that calls the specific module, right next to the `source` argument. Option A is incorrect; `variables.tf` defines input variables for the module. Option B is incorrect; `required_providers` specifies provider versions, not module versions. Option D is incorrect; `terraform.tfvars` sets variable values, not module source versions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_4_16',
  'After changing a module''s `version` constraint in your configuration, which Terraform command must you run to download the newly specified module version?',
  'mcq',
  '["`terraform init -upgrade`","`terraform plan`","`terraform apply`","`terraform validate`"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (`terraform init -upgrade`) is correct. When you change a module''s version constraint, `terraform init` needs to be run again to download the new version. The `-upgrade` flag specifically tells Terraform to check for and download newer versions that satisfy the constraints, even if a compatible version is already cached. Option B (`terraform plan`) and Option C (`terraform apply`) will use the currently downloaded module. Option D (`terraform validate`) only checks syntax and configuration validity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_4_17',
  'Why is it considered a best practice to pin module versions in production Terraform configurations?',
  'mcq',
  '["To reduce the number of files Terraform needs to download during `init`.","To prevent unexpected breaking changes or regressions from new module releases.","To ensure that all modules are always running the absolute latest features.","To force Terraform to re-download modules on every `terraform init`."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the primary reason for pinning module versions. It provides stability and predictability by ensuring that your infrastructure is always deployed with a known, tested version of a module, avoiding unintended side effects from upstream changes. Option A is incorrect; pinning versions doesn''t necessarily reduce download size. Option C is incorrect; while new features are good, stability is paramount in production, and updates should be controlled. Option D is incorrect; pinning versions does not force re-download on every `init` if the version is already cached.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_4_18',
  'What is the purpose of the `version` argument in a Terraform `module` block when sourcing from the Terraform Registry?',
  'mcq',
  '["To specify the Terraform CLI version required to use the module.","To define the API version of the cloud provider used by the module.","To constrain which versions of the module Terraform is allowed to download.","To indicate the operating system version on which the module should run."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the purpose of the `version` argument. It allows you to specify version constraints (e.g., `~> 1.0`, `>= 1.0.0`) to control which versions of the module Terraform can download and use, preventing unexpected breaking changes. Option A refers to the `terraform` block''s `required_version`. Option B is related to provider configuration, not module versioning. Option D is irrelevant to module versioning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_4_19',
  'What is the primary risk of not specifying a `version` argument for a module sourced from the public Terraform Registry in a production environment?',
  'mcq',
  '["Terraform will fail to initialize the module.","The module will always download the oldest available version.","Future `terraform init` runs may download a new, potentially breaking, module version.","The module will consume more cloud resources than intended."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) describes the primary risk, which is a common anti-pattern (`ap-5.1-001`). Without a version constraint, Terraform will default to downloading the latest available version of the module during `terraform init`. This can lead to unexpected behavior, breaking changes, or regressions in production if a new module version introduces incompatible changes. Option A is incorrect; Terraform will initialize, but with the latest version. Option B is incorrect; it downloads the latest, not the oldest. Option D is irrelevant to module versioning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  66,
