INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_11',
  'What does ''resource drift'' refer to in the context of Terraform?',
  'mcq',
  '["The gradual increase in cloud resource costs over time.","The difference between the actual infrastructure and what is defined in the Terraform state file.","The process of migrating resources from one cloud provider to another.","The movement of Terraform configuration files between different environments."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately defines resource drift. It occurs when changes are made to infrastructure resources directly in the cloud provider''s console or API, outside of Terraform, causing the real-world state to diverge from the Terraform state file. Option A is a cost management concern, not drift. Option C is a migration process. Option D refers to configuration management, not resource drift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_12',
  'Which Terraform command is primarily used to detect resource drift by comparing the current state with the real-world infrastructure?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform apply","terraform init","terraform refresh","terraform validate"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is `terraform refresh`. This command updates the state file to reflect the current settings of the real-world infrastructure, thereby detecting any drift that has occurred. It does not make any changes to the infrastructure itself. Option A, `terraform apply`, makes changes. Option B, `terraform init`, initializes the working directory. Option D, `terraform validate`, checks configuration syntax.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_13',
  'What is an ''out-of-band'' change in the context of Terraform?',
  'mcq',
  '["A change made to the Terraform configuration files by an unauthorized user.","A change made to the infrastructure resources directly through the cloud provider''s console or API, bypassing Terraform.","A change that requires a new version of the Terraform CLI to be installed.","A change that is not supported by the current cloud provider."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) defines an ''out-of-band'' change. This refers to any modification made to infrastructure resources (e.g., changing an EC2 instance type, deleting a security group) using tools other than Terraform, such as the AWS Management Console, Azure Portal, or cloud provider CLIs. These changes cause resource drift. Option A is a security breach, not an out-of-band change in this context. Option C is a tooling update. Option D is a limitation of the cloud provider.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_14',
  'After detecting resource drift with `terraform plan`, what is the recommended action to reconcile the state with the configuration?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually revert the out-of-band changes in the cloud console.","Run `terraform destroy` to rebuild the entire infrastructure.","Run `terraform apply` to apply the changes necessary to bring the infrastructure back in line with the configuration.","Modify the Terraform configuration to match the drifted state."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the recommended action. After `terraform plan` shows the drift, running `terraform apply` will execute the necessary operations to bring the real infrastructure back into alignment with what is defined in the Terraform configuration and recorded in the state file. Option A is a manual process that defeats the purpose of Infrastructure as Code. Option B is destructive and generally not the goal. Option D might be necessary if the out-of-band change was intentional and needs to be codified, but `apply` is for reconciling existing drift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_15',
  'What is the primary purpose of the `terraform state` commands?',
  'mcq',
  '["To manage Terraform workspaces.","To directly manipulate the Terraform state file, such as moving or removing resources.","To import existing infrastructure into Terraform state.","To configure remote backends for state storage."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the primary purpose of the `terraform state` subcommand family. These commands (`state mv`, `state rm`, `state show`, `state list`, etc.) are used for advanced manipulation of the state file, allowing users to manage individual resources within the state. Option A is handled by `terraform workspace`. Option C is handled by `terraform import`. Option D is handled by the `backend` block and `terraform init`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_16',
  'Which command can be used to import an existing cloud resource into Terraform state?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform import","terraform attach","terraform acquire","terraform link"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is `terraform import`. This command allows you to bring existing infrastructure resources that were not created by Terraform under Terraform''s management by adding them to the state file. Option B, C, and D are not standard Terraform commands for this purpose.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_17',
  'A user manually deleted an Amazon S3 bucket that was managed by Terraform. What will `terraform plan` report when run next?',
  'mcq',
  '["It will report no changes, as the resource is gone.","It will propose to recreate the Amazon S3 bucket.","It will show an error indicating the state file is corrupted.","It will prompt the user to manually update the state file."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is what `terraform plan` will propose. Since the S3 bucket was deleted manually (an out-of-band change), Terraform''s state file still believes the bucket exists. When `terraform plan` runs, it will detect that the resource defined in the configuration and state is missing in the real world and will propose to recreate it to align the infrastructure with the desired state. Option A is incorrect; it will detect the discrepancy. Option C is incorrect; the state file itself is not corrupted, just out of sync. Option D is incorrect; Terraform proposes actions, it doesn''t prompt for manual state updates in this scenario.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_18',
  'What is the primary benefit of regularly detecting and reconciling resource drift?',
  'mcq',
  '["To reduce the overall cost of cloud infrastructure.","To ensure that the infrastructure accurately reflects the desired state defined in the Terraform configuration.","To prevent unauthorized access to sensitive data in the state file.","To speed up the execution time of Terraform commands."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the primary benefit. Regularly detecting and reconciling drift ensures that your actual infrastructure matches your Infrastructure as Code definition, maintaining consistency, predictability, and auditability. This is crucial for reliable and repeatable deployments. Option A is a potential side effect but not the primary purpose. Option C is a security concern addressed by other means (e.g., encryption, access control). Option D is not directly related to drift reconciliation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_19',
  'If a resource is removed from the Terraform configuration file but still exists in the cloud, what command should be used to remove it from the state file without destroying the actual resource?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform destroy","terraform state rm","terraform untrack","terraform delete"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is `terraform state rm`. This command allows you to remove a resource''s entry from the Terraform state file without destroying the actual resource in the cloud. This is useful when you want to stop managing a resource with Terraform but keep it running. Option A, `terraform destroy`, would destroy the resource. Option C and D are not standard Terraform commands for this purpose.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_20',
  'A team wants to ensure that their Terraform state always reflects the most up-to-date configuration of their AWS infrastructure, even if manual changes occur. Which Terraform command should they integrate into their CI/CD pipeline before `terraform plan`?',
  'mcq',
  '["terraform init","terraform apply -refresh=false","terraform refresh","terraform validate"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is `terraform refresh`. While `terraform plan` implicitly performs a refresh, explicitly running `terraform refresh` before `terraform plan` ensures that the state file is fully synchronized with the real-world infrastructure. This explicitly updates the state file, making `terraform plan`''s output more accurate regarding any drift. Option A, `terraform init`, initializes the backend and plugins. Option B, `terraform apply -refresh=false`, would prevent drift detection during apply. Option D, `terraform validate`, checks configuration syntax.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_21',
  'What does ''resource drift'' refer to in the context of Terraform?',
  'mcq',
  '["The process of migrating resources from one AWS region to another.","Changes made to infrastructure resources manually or by other tools outside of Terraform.","The natural aging and deprecation of cloud resources over time.","The difference in cost between planned and actual infrastructure deployments."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately defines resource drift. It occurs when changes are applied to infrastructure resources (e.g., via the AWS Management Console, CLI, or another automation tool) that are not reflected in the Terraform configuration or state file. This leads to a discrepancy between the desired state (in configuration) and the actual state (in the cloud). Option A (incorrect) describes a migration process. Option C (incorrect) refers to resource lifecycle, not drift. Option D (incorrect) relates to cost management, not resource drift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_22',
  'After running `terraform refresh`, what is the next logical step to understand the impact of any detected drift and plan for reconciliation?',
  'mcq',
  '["Run `terraform apply --auto-approve` to fix the drift immediately.","Manually update the configuration files to match the drifted resources.","Run `terraform plan` to see what changes Terraform would propose to bring the infrastructure back to the desired state.","Delete the state file and re-import all resources."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the next logical step. After `terraform refresh` updates the state file with the current reality, `terraform plan` will then compare this refreshed state against your configuration files. It will show you a plan of actions (add, change, destroy) that Terraform would take to align the real infrastructure with your configuration, effectively showing how to reconcile the drift. Option A (incorrect) applying changes without reviewing the plan can lead to unintended consequences. Option B (incorrect) manually updating configuration to match drift is an anti-pattern; configuration should represent the desired state. Option D (incorrect) deleting and re-importing is an extreme measure and generally not the first step for drift reconciliation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_23',
  'A developer wants to remove a specific resource from Terraform''s management without destroying the actual resource in the cloud. Which command should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["`terraform destroy <resource_address>`","`terraform state rm <resource_address>`","`terraform untrack <resource_address>`","`terraform remove <resource_address>`"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) `terraform state rm <resource_address>` is the command used to remove a resource from the Terraform state file. This means Terraform will no longer manage that resource, but the actual resource in the cloud will remain untouched. Option A (incorrect) `terraform destroy` would destroy the actual resource in the cloud. Option C and D are not valid Terraform commands for this purpose.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_24',
  'Which Terraform command allows you to bring existing, unmanaged infrastructure under Terraform''s control by adding it to the state file?',
  'mcq',
  '["`terraform init`","`terraform import`","`terraform attach`","`terraform adopt`"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) `terraform import` is the command used to bring existing infrastructure resources that were not created by Terraform into the Terraform state file. This allows Terraform to manage these resources going forward. Option A (incorrect) `terraform init` initializes the working directory and backend. Option C and D are not valid Terraform commands for this purpose.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_25',
  'When `terraform plan` is executed, what role does the Terraform state file play in generating the execution plan?',
  'mcq',
  '["It is ignored; `terraform plan` only compares configuration to real infrastructure.","It serves as the baseline, representing the current known state of the managed infrastructure.","It defines the desired state of the infrastructure, overriding the configuration files.","It stores the history of all previous `terraform plan` executions."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes the role of the state file. `terraform plan` compares the desired state (defined in the configuration files) against the current known state (recorded in the state file) and the actual state of the infrastructure (queried from the cloud provider). The state file acts as the authoritative record of what Terraform believes it has deployed. Option A (incorrect) the state file is crucial for `terraform plan`. Option C (incorrect) the configuration files define the desired state, not the state file itself. Option D (incorrect) the state file does not store the history of `terraform plan` executions; it stores the actual resource state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_26',
  'A team wants to ensure that their Terraform configuration always reflects the current state of their infrastructure before proposing changes. Which command should they integrate into their CI/CD pipeline before `terraform plan`?',
  'mcq',
  '["`terraform init`","`terraform validate`","`terraform refresh`","`terraform output`"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) `terraform refresh` updates the state file to reflect the current real-world state of the infrastructure. Running this before `terraform plan` ensures that the plan is based on the most up-to-date information, accurately detecting any drift that may have occurred. Option A (incorrect) `terraform init` initializes the working directory and backend. Option B (incorrect) `terraform validate` checks configuration syntax. Option D (incorrect) `terraform output` displays output values.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_27',
  'What is the primary risk of not regularly detecting and addressing resource drift?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increased cloud provider costs due to unmanaged resources.","Terraform state file becoming too large and difficult to manage.","Loss of confidence in Terraform as the source of truth, leading to unexpected changes or failures during `terraform apply`.","Automatic deletion of resources that are not defined in the Terraform configuration."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the primary risk. If drift is not detected and addressed, the Terraform state file no longer accurately reflects the infrastructure. This can lead to a loss of confidence in Terraform''s ability to manage the environment, and subsequent `terraform apply` operations might produce unexpected results, attempt to revert manual changes, or even fail because the actual infrastructure doesn''t match the expected state. Option A (incorrect) while unmanaged resources can increase costs, this is a consequence of drift, not the primary risk of not addressing it. Option B (incorrect) state file size is generally not a primary concern for drift. Option D (incorrect) Terraform does not automatically delete resources it doesn''t manage; it only manages what''s in its state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_28',
  'To prevent accidental destruction of critical resources, a team wants to ensure that a specific database instance cannot be destroyed by Terraform. Which lifecycle argument should be added to the database resource block?',
  'mcq',
  '["`ignore_changes = all`","`prevent_destroy = true`","`create_before_destroy = true`","`no_destroy = true`"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) `prevent_destroy = true` is a lifecycle argument that, when set, causes Terraform to error out if a plan includes destroying the resource. This acts as a safeguard against accidental deletion of critical infrastructure. Option A (incorrect) `ignore_changes = all` prevents Terraform from detecting changes to the resource, but not from destroying it. Option C (incorrect) `create_before_destroy = true` changes the order of operations when a resource needs to be replaced. Option D (incorrect) `no_destroy = true` is not a valid lifecycle argument.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_29',
  'A team has manually created an Amazon S3 bucket outside of Terraform. They now want to manage this bucket using Terraform. Which command should they use to bring this existing bucket into their Terraform state?',
  'mcq',
  '["`terraform get s3_bucket.my_bucket`","`terraform import aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket existing-bucket-name`","`terraform add aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket`","`terraform sync aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket`"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) `terraform import aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket existing-bucket-name` is the correct command. `terraform import` requires the Terraform resource address (e.g., `aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket`) and the ID of the real-world resource (e.g., `existing-bucket-name`). This command adds the existing resource to the Terraform state file so it can be managed by Terraform. Option A, C, and D are not valid Terraform commands for importing resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_30',
  'What is the consequence of manually modifying a resource that is managed by Terraform, without updating the Terraform configuration or state?',
  'mcq',
  '["Terraform will automatically detect the change and update the configuration files.","The resource will be immediately destroyed by Terraform upon the next `terraform plan`.","The resource will experience drift, and subsequent `terraform plan` operations may propose reverting the manual change or applying unexpected updates.","Terraform will ignore the resource entirely and cease to manage it."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) describes the consequence. Manually modifying a Terraform-managed resource causes resource drift. When `terraform plan` is run next, it will compare the configuration (desired state) with the actual state (which now includes the manual change) and the state file. It will then propose actions to reconcile this difference, which often means reverting the manual change or making other unexpected modifications to bring the resource back into alignment with the configuration. Option A (incorrect) Terraform does not automatically update configuration files. Option B (incorrect) `terraform plan` does not destroy resources; it only proposes changes. Option D (incorrect) Terraform will still manage the resource, but its understanding of it will be outdated.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_31',
  'What does ''resource drift'' refer to in the context of Terraform?',
  'mcq',
  '["Changes in the Terraform configuration files that have not yet been applied.","The process of migrating resources from one cloud provider to another.","Discrepancies between the actual state of infrastructure resources and the state recorded in Terraform''s state file.","The automatic scaling of resources based on demand."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) precisely defines resource drift. It occurs when changes are made to infrastructure resources outside of Terraform (e.g., manual changes via the AWS Management Console, Azure Portal, or CLI), causing the actual deployed infrastructure to diverge from what Terraform believes it has provisioned (as recorded in the `terraform.tfstate` file). Option A (incorrect) describes pending changes, not drift. Option B (incorrect) refers to cloud migration. Option D (incorrect) describes auto-scaling, which is a dynamic resource management feature, not a state management issue.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  95,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_32',
  'Which Terraform command is used to reconcile the Terraform state file with the actual infrastructure by reading the current settings of all managed remote objects?',
  'mcq',
  '["`terraform apply`","`terraform validate`","`terraform plan`","`terraform refresh`"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is `terraform refresh`. This command is specifically designed to update the state file to reflect the real-world state of your infrastructure. It queries the cloud provider for the current attributes of all resources managed by the configuration and updates the `terraform.tfstate` file accordingly. Option A (`terraform apply`) applies changes to the infrastructure based on the configuration and state. Option B (`terraform validate`) checks the syntax and configuration validity. Option C (`terraform plan`) generates an execution plan by comparing the configuration, state, and real-world infrastructure, and implicitly performs a refresh unless explicitly told not to.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  96,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_33',
  'How does `terraform plan` help in detecting resource drift?',
  'mcq',
  '["It explicitly runs `terraform refresh` and then compares the refreshed state with the configuration to show proposed changes.","It only validates the syntax of the Terraform configuration files without interacting with the remote state.","It directly modifies the remote infrastructure to match the configuration, thus resolving drift.","It creates a new, separate state file that reflects the current infrastructure, leaving the original state untouched."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately describes how `terraform plan` detects drift. By default, `terraform plan` first performs a refresh (reads the current state of resources from the cloud provider) and then compares this refreshed state against the desired state defined in the configuration. Any differences between the refreshed state and the configuration are presented as proposed changes in the plan, indicating drift. Option B (incorrect) describes `terraform validate`. Option C (incorrect) describes `terraform apply`. Option D (incorrect) is not how `terraform plan` operates; it works with the existing state file.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  97,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_34',
  'After detecting resource drift where a manually deleted Amazon S3 bucket (managed by Terraform) is no longer present, what is the most straightforward way to reconcile the Terraform state file with the actual infrastructure?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually recreate the S3 bucket through the AWS Management Console.","Run `terraform apply` to instruct Terraform to recreate the missing S3 bucket based on the configuration.","Manually edit the `terraform.tfstate` file to remove the entry for the deleted S3 bucket.","Execute `terraform destroy` to remove all other resources and then start over."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most straightforward and recommended way to reconcile the state. When `terraform apply` is run after a resource has been manually deleted, Terraform will detect that the resource is missing (via an implicit refresh) and, seeing it defined in the configuration, will propose to recreate it. If this is the desired outcome, applying the plan will bring the infrastructure back into alignment with the configuration. Option A (incorrect) is a manual intervention that defeats the purpose of Infrastructure as Code and could lead to further drift if not perfectly matched. Option C (incorrect) is highly risky and generally discouraged, as manual state file edits can easily corrupt the state. Option D (incorrect) is an extreme measure that would destroy all managed infrastructure, which is rarely the desired outcome for a single missing resource.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  98,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_35',
  'Which of the following is considered a best practice to minimize resource drift in a Terraform-managed environment?',
  'mcq',
  '["Granting all team members full administrative access to the cloud provider console for quick fixes.","Relying solely on manual `terraform refresh` commands to detect changes periodically.","Implementing strict access controls and policies to ensure all infrastructure changes are made exclusively through Terraform.","Regularly backing up the Terraform state file to a local machine."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most effective best practice for minimizing resource drift. By enforcing strict access controls and policies (e.g., using IAM policies in AWS, Azure RBAC, GCP IAM) that restrict direct manual modifications to infrastructure resources, organizations ensure that Terraform remains the single source of truth for infrastructure changes. This prevents out-of-band changes that cause drift. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern that significantly increases the risk of drift and security vulnerabilities. Option B (incorrect) is a reactive measure for detecting drift, not preventing it. Option D (incorrect) is a good practice for disaster recovery but does not prevent drift from occurring.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  99,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

