INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_90',
  'A developer has made several changes to a Terraform configuration and wants to ensure that the code is consistently formatted before pushing to a shared repository. They also want to see the exact changes `terraform fmt` would make without applying them directly, to understand the impact on the file. Order the commands to achieve this.',
  'ordering',
  '["Review the diff output to understand the formatting changes.","Run `terraform fmt -diff -check` to preview formatting changes.","Execute `terraform fmt` to apply the canonical formatting.","Save the modified Terraform configuration files."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'To ensure consistent formatting and review changes, a specific sequence of actions is followed. Option D (Save the modified Terraform configuration files) is the initial step after making any changes. Option B (Run `terraform fmt -diff -check` to preview formatting changes) allows the developer to see what changes `terraform fmt` would make without actually writing them to the files, and the `-check` flag ensures it exits with a non-zero status if unformatted. Option A (Review the diff output to understand the formatting changes) is the logical next step after generating the diff. Finally, Option C (Execute `terraform fmt` to apply the canonical formatting) is run to actually modify the files to conform to the standard style. Running `terraform fmt` directly without `-diff -check` would apply changes without a preview, which might be undesirable.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_91',
  'A new Terraform module has been developed by a junior engineer. Before it can be used in production configurations, the lead engineer needs to review its structure and ensure it adheres to the team''s coding standards and Terraform''s canonical formatting. Order the steps the lead engineer should take to automatically format the module and then verify its consistency.',
  'ordering',
  '["Run `terraform validate` to check for syntax and internal consistency.","Navigate to the module''s root directory.","Execute `terraform fmt -recursive` to apply canonical formatting.","Run `terraform init` to download any necessary providers or modules."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'When reviewing and preparing a new Terraform module, a structured approach ensures quality. Option B (Navigate to the module''s root directory) is the initial contextual step. Option C (Execute `terraform fmt -recursive` to apply canonical formatting) should be performed first to ensure the code adheres to a consistent style, which aids readability and review. Option D (Run `terraform init` to download any necessary providers or modules) is crucial next, as validation and planning require the working directory to be properly initialized. Finally, Option A (Run `terraform validate` to check for syntax and internal consistency) verifies the correctness of the configuration without deploying anything. Attempting to validate or initialize before formatting might lead to inconsistencies or errors being harder to spot.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_7_92',
  'A DevOps team is setting up a new CI/CD pipeline for their Terraform configurations. A critical requirement is to ensure that all committed code is not only syntactically correct but also adheres to the team''s formatting standards. The pipeline should fail early if any formatting issues are detected. Order the steps in the CI/CD pipeline to implement this robust code quality check.',
  'ordering',
  '["Run `terraform init` to prepare the working directory.","Execute `terraform fmt -check -recursive` to verify formatting.","Perform `terraform validate` to check configuration validity.","Clone the Terraform repository from version control."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'In a CI/CD pipeline, the sequence of checks is optimized for early failure detection and efficiency. Option D (Clone the Terraform repository from version control) is the absolute first step to get the code. Option B (Execute `terraform fmt -check -recursive` to verify formatting) should be the next step, as it''s a quick check that doesn''t require provider downloads or backend configuration, failing early if formatting is incorrect. Option A (Run `terraform init` to prepare the working directory) must follow to download providers and set up the backend, which is a prerequisite for validation. Finally, Option C (Perform `terraform validate` to check configuration validity) is executed to ensure the configuration is syntactically correct and internally consistent. Running `terraform init` before `terraform fmt -check` is less efficient as `fmt -check` doesn''t need initialization, and running `terraform validate` before `terraform init` would fail.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_7',
  'hard'
);

