INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_105',
  'A senior DevOps engineer is tasked with provisioning a new set of critical infrastructure resources for a production environment using an existing Terraform configuration. The team requires strict adherence to best practices, including explicit initialization, configuration validation, a thorough review of proposed changes, and a final approval step before any infrastructure modifications are made. Arrange the following Terraform CLI commands in the correct sequence to achieve this, ensuring a saved execution plan is used for the apply operation.',
  'ordering',
  '["Execute `terraform apply \"tfplan\"` to provision the resources.","Run `terraform plan -out=\"tfplan\"` to generate and save an execution plan.","Initialize the working directory and backend with `terraform init`.","Validate the configuration files for syntax and consistency using `terraform validate`."]'::json,
  '{2,3,1,0}',
  'The standard and recommended Terraform workflow for provisioning infrastructure involves a specific sequence of commands to ensure safety and predictability. Option C (Initialize the working directory and backend with `terraform init`) is the first step, as it downloads necessary providers and sets up the backend for state management. Option D (Validate the configuration files for syntax and consistency using `terraform validate`) should follow initialization to catch any syntax errors or internal inconsistencies before attempting to generate a plan. Option B (Run `terraform plan -out="tfplan"` to generate and save an execution plan) comes next, creating a detailed preview of changes and saving it to a file, which is crucial for review and consistent application. Finally, Option A (Execute `terraform apply "tfplan"` to provision the resources) uses the saved plan to make the actual infrastructure changes, ensuring that only the reviewed and approved changes are applied. Running `terraform apply` without a saved plan directly after `terraform validate` would bypass the explicit plan review step, which is a common anti-pattern for production environments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_106',
  'A development team is preparing to deploy a new microservice to a staging environment using Terraform. They have just pulled the latest configuration changes from version control. Before proceeding with any infrastructure modifications, they need to ensure their local environment is correctly set up, the configuration is syntactically correct, and they can preview the changes without immediately applying them. Order the following steps to reflect the standard, safe Terraform workflow for this scenario.',
  'ordering',
  '["Run `terraform apply` to create or update resources.","Execute `terraform plan` to preview the proposed infrastructure changes.","Initialize the Terraform working directory with `terraform init`.","Validate the Terraform configuration files using `terraform validate`."]'::json,
  '{2,3,1,0}',
  'The standard Terraform workflow is designed to be safe and predictable. Option C (Initialize the Terraform working directory with `terraform init`) is always the first step after cloning a new repository or changing backend configurations, as it downloads providers and sets up the state backend. Option D (Validate the Terraform configuration files using `terraform validate`) should follow to check for syntax errors and internal consistency without interacting with the cloud provider. Option B (Execute `terraform plan` to preview the proposed infrastructure changes) is then used to see exactly what Terraform will do before any actual changes are made. Finally, Option A (Run `terraform apply` to create or update resources) is the command to execute the changes, typically after reviewing the plan. Skipping `terraform init` or `terraform validate` can lead to errors, and skipping `terraform plan` can lead to unexpected infrastructure changes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_107',
  'A new Terraform configuration has been developed to manage a complex application''s infrastructure. Before sharing it with the wider team or integrating it into a CI/CD pipeline, the lead engineer wants to ensure the configuration adheres to best practices for readability, consistency, and correctness. Order the following actions to prepare the configuration for review and deployment.',
  'ordering',
  '["Execute `terraform plan` to generate a preview of infrastructure changes.","Run `terraform fmt -recursive` to apply canonical formatting to all `.tf` files.","Initialize the Terraform working directory with `terraform init`.","Perform a `terraform validate` to check for syntax and consistency errors."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'To prepare a new Terraform configuration for team collaboration and deployment, a logical sequence of steps ensures quality and correctness. Option B (Run `terraform fmt -recursive` to apply canonical formatting to all `.tf` files) should be the first step to ensure consistent code style, which is crucial for readability and maintainability across a team. Option C (Initialize the Terraform working directory with `terraform init`) is necessary next to download providers and set up the backend, which are prerequisites for validation and planning. Option D (Perform a `terraform validate` to check for syntax and consistency errors) should follow initialization to ensure the configuration is syntactically correct and internally consistent before interacting with the cloud provider. Finally, Option A (Execute `terraform plan` to generate a preview of infrastructure changes) allows the engineer to see the proposed changes, confirming the configuration''s intended effect. Running `terraform plan` before `terraform init` or `terraform validate` would likely result in errors, and skipping `terraform fmt` would lead to inconsistent code style.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_108',
  'A company is migrating an existing application to AWS and needs to manage its infrastructure using Terraform. The application requires a new Amazon VPC, subnets, and security groups. The DevOps team wants to follow the standard Terraform workflow to provision these resources, ensuring that the configuration is valid and changes are reviewed before being applied. Arrange the following high-level steps in the correct order for this initial infrastructure setup.',
  'ordering',
  '["Apply the planned changes to create the AWS resources.","Initialize the Terraform working directory.","Validate the Terraform configuration for syntax and consistency.","Generate an execution plan to preview the infrastructure changes."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'The core Terraform workflow ensures a predictable and safe approach to infrastructure management. Option B (Initialize the Terraform working directory) is the essential first step, as `terraform init` downloads necessary providers and configures the backend. Option C (Validate the Terraform configuration for syntax and consistency) should follow to catch any errors in the `.tf` files before attempting to interact with the cloud provider. Option D (Generate an execution plan to preview the infrastructure changes) is critical for reviewing what Terraform intends to do, allowing for verification before modification. Finally, Option A (Apply the planned changes to create the AWS resources) executes the plan, provisioning the infrastructure. Deviating from this order, such as applying before planning or validating, can lead to unexpected errors or unintended infrastructure changes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_109',
  'A team is managing a critical production environment with Terraform. They need to update a specific resource within their existing configuration, but they want to ensure minimal disruption and a clear understanding of the changes. After modifying the `.tf` files, they must follow a strict process of verification and approval. Order the following actions to correctly apply the changes to the infrastructure.',
  'ordering',
  '["Execute `terraform apply` to provision the changes, confirming interactively.","Run `terraform init -upgrade` to ensure all providers and modules are up-to-date.","Perform `terraform validate` to check for configuration syntax and consistency.","Generate and review an execution plan using `terraform plan`."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'For managing a production environment, a rigorous workflow is essential. Option B (Run `terraform init -upgrade` to ensure all providers and modules are up-to-date) is often a good first step after configuration changes or pulling updates, ensuring the local environment matches the configuration''s requirements. Option C (Perform `terraform validate` to check for configuration syntax and consistency) is crucial to catch any errors before proceeding. Option D (Generate and review an execution plan using `terraform plan`) allows the team to understand exactly what changes will be made, which is vital for critical environments. Finally, Option A (Execute `terraform apply` to provision the changes, confirming interactively) applies the changes, with interactive confirmation providing a final safety check. Applying changes without validating or planning first is a significant risk in production.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_1_110',
  'A team is developing a new feature that requires temporary infrastructure. After successfully provisioning and testing the feature, the team needs to completely remove all associated resources to avoid unnecessary costs. They want to ensure the deletion process is thorough and follows best practices for a controlled teardown. Order the following steps to safely destroy the Terraform-managed infrastructure.',
  'ordering',
  '["Run `terraform destroy` and confirm the operation interactively.","Execute `terraform init` to ensure the working directory is properly configured.","Perform `terraform validate` to confirm the configuration is still valid.","Generate a destroy plan using `terraform plan -destroy` to preview resources to be deleted."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'To safely destroy Terraform-managed infrastructure, a methodical approach is required. Option B (Execute `terraform init` to ensure the working directory is properly configured) is the initial step to ensure all providers and the backend are correctly set up, especially if the repository was recently cloned or updated. Option C (Perform `terraform validate` to confirm the configuration is still valid) should follow to catch any syntax or consistency issues that might prevent Terraform from correctly identifying resources. Option D (Generate a destroy plan using `terraform plan -destroy` to preview resources to be deleted) is a critical safety measure, allowing the team to review exactly which resources will be destroyed before proceeding. Finally, Option A (Run `terraform destroy` and confirm the operation interactively) executes the destruction, with interactive confirmation serving as a final safeguard. Skipping the plan review (`terraform plan -destroy`) can lead to accidental deletion of unintended resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_1',
  'hard'
);

