INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_48',
  'A development team is tasked with setting up a new Terraform project from scratch to manage cloud resources for a new application. They need to ensure the project is properly initialized, the configuration is valid, and they can preview changes before applying them. Order the following steps to establish a new Terraform project and prepare for its first deployment.',
  'ordering',
  '["Write the initial Terraform configuration files (`.tf`).","Run `terraform plan` to preview the infrastructure changes.","Execute `terraform init` to initialize the working directory.","Perform `terraform validate` to check for syntax and consistency errors."]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}',
  'When starting a new Terraform project, a logical sequence ensures a smooth setup. Option A (Write the initial Terraform configuration files (`.tf`)) is the very first step, as you need code to manage. Option C (Execute `terraform init` to initialize the working directory) must follow to download providers and set up the backend based on the written configuration. Option D (Perform `terraform validate` to check for syntax and consistency errors) is then used to ensure the configuration is correct before attempting to interact with the cloud provider. Finally, Option B (Run `terraform plan` to preview the infrastructure changes) allows for a review of what Terraform intends to do. Attempting to initialize, validate, or plan without any configuration files would be meaningless or result in errors.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  109,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_49',
  'A Terraform project has been migrated to a new remote backend configuration. The team needs to reconfigure their local working directories to point to this new backend and ensure all necessary plugins are downloaded. Order the steps for a developer to correctly update their local environment to reflect the new backend configuration.',
  'ordering',
  '["Run `terraform plan` to verify the configuration against the new backend.","Update the backend configuration block in the `.tf` files.","Execute `terraform init -reconfigure` to switch to the new backend.","Perform `terraform validate` to check the updated configuration."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'When migrating to a new remote backend, a specific sequence is required to ensure a smooth transition. Option B (Update the backend configuration block in the `.tf` files) is the first logical step, as the configuration must reflect the new backend details. Option C (Execute `terraform init -reconfigure` to switch to the new backend) is then used to force Terraform to forget any previous backend configuration and initialize with the new one, downloading any new providers if necessary. Option D (Perform `terraform validate` to check the updated configuration) should follow to ensure the configuration is still valid with the new backend context. Finally, Option A (Run `terraform plan` to verify the configuration against the new backend) allows for a preview of changes, confirming that Terraform can correctly interact with the new state. Running `terraform init -reconfigure` before updating the backend configuration would attempt to reconfigure with the old, incorrect settings.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  110,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_50',
  'A developer has pulled a new version of a Terraform configuration that includes updates to provider versions and new modules. Their local working directory is outdated. They need to ensure all local plugins and modules are updated to match the configuration''s requirements without affecting the backend state. Order the steps to correctly update the local Terraform working directory.',
  'ordering',
  '["Run `terraform validate` to ensure the updated configuration is valid.","Execute `terraform init -upgrade` to download new provider versions and modules.","Review the updated provider and module versions in the `.terraform` directory.","Pull the latest Terraform configuration files from version control."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'To update a local Terraform working directory after pulling new configuration changes, a specific sequence ensures all components are refreshed. Option D (Pull the latest Terraform configuration files from version control) is the essential first step to get the updated `.tf` files. Option B (Execute `terraform init -upgrade` to download new provider versions and modules) should follow, as the `-upgrade` flag forces Terraform to update to the newest available versions of providers and modules that satisfy the configuration''s constraints. Option A (Run `terraform validate` to ensure the updated configuration is valid) is then used to check for any syntax or consistency issues with the newly updated configuration and providers. Finally, Option C (Review the updated provider and module versions in the `.terraform` directory) allows for verification that the updates were successful. Running `terraform init -upgrade` before pulling the latest configuration would update based on potentially old requirements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  111,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_51',
  'A team is developing a complex Terraform configuration that uses several community-provided modules. They''ve recently identified a bug in one of these modules and need to temporarily switch to a specific, older version of that module to avoid the bug, while keeping other modules and providers updated. Order the steps to achieve this precise module version management.',
  'ordering',
  '["Update the module''s `source` block in the configuration to specify the older version.","Run `terraform init -upgrade` to download the specified older module version and update others.","Execute `terraform validate` to ensure the configuration is consistent with the older module.","Review the `.terraform/modules` directory to confirm the correct module version is present."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To manage specific module versions, a precise sequence of actions is required. Option A (Update the module''s `source` block in the configuration to specify the older version) is the initial and critical step to declare the desired module version. Option B (Run `terraform init -upgrade` to download the specified older module version and update others) should follow, as `init -upgrade` will fetch the exact version specified for the module and update other components as needed. Option C (Execute `terraform validate` to ensure the configuration is consistent with the older module) is then used to check for any compatibility or syntax issues that might arise from using an older module version. Finally, Option D (Review the `.terraform/modules` directory to confirm the correct module version is present) allows for direct verification of the installed module. Running `terraform init -upgrade` before updating the `source` block would not achieve the desired module version change.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  112,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_52',
  'A team is working on a legacy Terraform project where provider versions are not explicitly pinned, leading to potential inconsistencies across developer environments. To standardize the environment and prevent unexpected behavior, they decide to explicitly define provider versions and then ensure all local setups reflect these pinned versions. Order the steps to implement and enforce this provider versioning.',
  'ordering',
  '["Run `terraform init` to download the newly pinned provider versions.","Add `required_providers` blocks with specific version constraints to the configuration.","Commit the updated configuration files to version control.","Verify the installed provider versions in the `.terraform/providers` directory."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To enforce provider versioning, a clear sequence of actions is necessary. Option B (Add `required_providers` blocks with specific version constraints to the configuration) is the initial and critical step to declare the desired provider versions. Option A (Run `terraform init` to download the newly pinned provider versions) must follow to ensure that Terraform fetches and uses the versions specified in the configuration. Option C (Commit the updated configuration files to version control) is essential to share these new constraints with the team. Finally, Option D (Verify the installed provider versions in the `.terraform/providers` directory) allows for confirmation that the correct versions are being used. Running `terraform init` before defining the `required_providers` block would not enforce the desired versioning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  113,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_53',
  'A developer is setting up a new Terraform project that will manage resources across multiple AWS regions. The project utilizes a custom local module for common networking components. Before writing the main configuration, the developer needs to ensure the local module is correctly referenced and initialized. Order the steps to prepare the working directory for this multi-region setup with a local module.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the local module source in the main configuration.","Create the local module''s `.tf` files in a dedicated subdirectory.","Execute `terraform init` to discover and download the local module.","Run `terraform validate` to check the main configuration and module syntax."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To set up a Terraform project with local modules, a specific order ensures proper discovery and initialization. Option B (Create the local module''s `.tf` files in a dedicated subdirectory) is the first step, as the module itself must exist. Option A (Define the local module source in the main configuration) is then required to tell the main configuration where to find the local module. Option C (Execute `terraform init` to discover and download the local module) is crucial next, as `init` is responsible for discovering and preparing modules, even local ones. Finally, Option D (Run `terraform validate` to check the main configuration and module syntax) ensures that both the main configuration and the local module are syntactically correct and consistent. Attempting to initialize or validate before the module files exist or are referenced would lead to errors.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain71_16',
  NOW(),
  114,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_71_2',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_71_m_71_2_54',
  'A new developer joins a project and needs to set up their local Terraform environment to work with an existing configuration. Order the steps they should take to prepare their working directory.',
  'ordering',
  '["Run `terraform plan` to verify the configuration.","Clone the Terraform configuration repository.","Execute `terraform init` in the cloned directory.","Ensure Terraform CLI is installed and configured."]'::json,
  '{3,1,2,0}',
  'Before anything else, the developer must ensure the Terraform CLI is installed and configured on their machine (Option D). Then, they need to clone the existing Terraform configuration repository (Option B). Once the repository is cloned, `terraform init` must be executed within the cloned directory to download providers and set up the backend (Option C). Finally, `terraform plan` can be run to verify the configuration and ensure it''s working as expected (Option A).',
