INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_3_49',
  'A developer is creating a new Terraform configuration and wants to use a module published on the public Terraform Registry. Which of the following elements are required within the `module` block to correctly reference and instantiate this module? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["A `source` argument specifying the module''s address","A `version` argument to pin the module''s release","A `name` attribute to identify the module instance","An `alias` attribute for multiple instances","A `count` argument for conditional creation"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (A `source` argument specifying the module''s address) is essential as it tells Terraform where to find the module, e.g., `hashicorp/vpc/aws`. Option C (A `name` attribute to identify the module instance) is also required, as it provides a local name for the module block, allowing it to be referenced within the configuration. Option B (A `version` argument to pin the module''s release) is highly recommended for production environments to prevent unexpected changes (anti-pattern ap-5.1-001) but is not strictly mandatory for Terraform to initialize the module. Option D (An `alias` attribute for multiple instances) is not a standard attribute for module blocks. Option E (A `count` argument for conditional creation) is a meta-argument for creating multiple instances of a module, but not a fundamental requirement for defining a single module instance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_3_50',
  'When calling a child module within a root module, which of the following are valid ways to pass configuration values from the root module to the child module? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["As arguments directly within the `module` block.","By defining `output` blocks in the root module with matching names.","Through `data` sources that reference the child module''s inputs.","By setting environment variables prefixed with `TF_VAR_` for the child module.","By referencing `resource` attributes from the root module directly."]'::json,
  '{0,4}',
  'Option A (As arguments directly within the `module` block) is the standard and most common way to pass values, e.g., `module "example" { input_var = "value" }`. Option E (By referencing `resource` attributes from the root module directly) is also a common pattern, where outputs from resources defined in the root module are passed as inputs to the child module, e.g., `module "example" { vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id }`. Option B (By defining `output` blocks in the root module with matching names) is incorrect; `output` blocks expose values from a module, they don''t pass inputs to a child module. Option C (Through `data` sources that reference the child module''s inputs) is incorrect; `data` sources retrieve information, they don''t pass inputs to modules. Option D (By setting environment variables prefixed with `TF_VAR_` for the child module) is incorrect; `TF_VAR_` environment variables apply to the root module''s variables, not directly to child module inputs unless those inputs are themselves passed from root module variables.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_3_51',
  'A Terraform configuration uses a module to create a network infrastructure. The root module needs to dynamically set the CIDR block for a subnet within this module. Which of the following actions must be taken? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Define a `variable` block in the child module to accept the CIDR block.","Pass the CIDR block as an argument within the `module` call block in the root module.","Define an `output` block in the child module to expose the CIDR block.","Use a `data` source in the root module to fetch the CIDR block.","Declare the CIDR block as a local value within the root module."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Define a `variable` block in the child module to accept the CIDR block) is necessary for the child module to declare that it expects an input for the CIDR block. Option B (Pass the CIDR block as an argument within the `module` call block in the root module) is how the root module provides the actual value for that variable to the child module. Option C (Define an `output` block in the child module to expose the CIDR block) is for the child module to return a value, not to receive an input. Option D (Use a `data` source in the root module to fetch the CIDR block) might provide the value, but it still needs to be passed to the child module via a variable. Option E (Declare the CIDR block as a local value within the root module) can define the value, but it still needs to be explicitly passed as an argument to the child module.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_3_52',
  'A Terraform configuration needs to instantiate the same child module multiple times, each with slightly different input parameters. Which of the following Terraform features can be used to achieve this efficiently? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Using the `for_each` meta-argument within the `module` block.","Using the `count` meta-argument within the `module` block.","Defining multiple `module` blocks with unique names.","Creating separate `.tf` files for each module instance.","Using `dynamic` blocks within the child module definition."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Using the `for_each` meta-argument within the `module` block) allows creating multiple instances of a module based on a map or set of strings, providing distinct configurations for each instance. Option B (Using the `count` meta-argument within the `module` block) allows creating multiple identical or slightly varied instances of a module based on an integer count. Option C (Defining multiple `module` blocks with unique names) is a valid but less efficient way to achieve this, as it requires duplicating the module call block for each instance. Option D (Creating separate `.tf` files for each module instance) is not a direct feature for instantiating modules multiple times, but rather an organizational choice. Option E (Using `dynamic` blocks within the child module definition) is for dynamically creating nested blocks within a resource or data source, not for instantiating the module itself multiple times.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_3_53',
  'A Terraform configuration needs to deploy a consistent set of AWS resources, including a VPC, subnets, and security groups, across multiple environments (dev, staging, prod). Which of the following approaches would be most effective for managing this consistency and reusability? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Encapsulating the network resources in a child module and calling it multiple times with different inputs.","Copying and pasting the resource definitions into separate `.tf` files for each environment.","Using `terraform workspace` to manage environment-specific configurations within a single root module.","Defining all resources directly in the root module and using `count` for each resource.","Storing the resource definitions in an Amazon S3 bucket and referencing them as data sources."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Encapsulating the network resources in a child module and calling it multiple times with different inputs) is a core principle of Terraform module usage, promoting reusability and consistency. The module can be called for each environment with specific variable values. Option C (Using `terraform workspace` to manage environment-specific configurations within a single root module) is also a valid approach for managing multiple environments from a single configuration, allowing for distinct state files and variable values per workspace. Option B (Copying and pasting the resource definitions into separate `.tf` files for each environment) leads to code duplication and makes maintenance difficult. Option D (Defining all resources directly in the root module and using `count` for each resource) would make the root module very large and complex, losing the benefits of modularity. Option E (Storing the resource definitions in an Amazon S3 bucket and referencing them as data sources) is incorrect; S3 can be a module source, but referencing them as data sources would not instantiate the resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_3_54',
  'A Terraform configuration calls a child module. Which of the following are valid ways for the root module to pass input values to this child module? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["By defining arguments directly within the `module` block.","By referencing `output` values from other resources in the root module.","By defining `variable` blocks in the root module that match the child module''s inputs.","By using `local` values defined in the child module.","By explicitly declaring the child module''s variables as `required`."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (By defining arguments directly within the `module` block) is the standard method to pass values, e.g., `module "my_module" { name = "value" }`. Option B (By referencing `output` values from other resources in the root module) is also a common pattern, allowing the output of one resource (e.g., `aws_vpc.main.id`) to become an input to a child module. Option C (By defining `variable` blocks in the root module that match the child module''s inputs) is incorrect; root module variables are inputs to the root module, they don''t automatically pass to child modules. Option D (By using `local` values defined in the child module) is incorrect; local values are internal to a module and cannot be used to pass inputs from a parent. Option E (By explicitly declaring the child module''s variables as `required`) is an attribute of a variable, not a mechanism for passing values.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_3_55',
  'When defining a `module` block in a Terraform configuration, which of the following are essential components or considerations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["A `source` argument specifying where the module code is located.","A unique name for the module instance within the configuration.","A `version` argument to specify the Terraform CLI version compatibility.","An `alias` attribute to create multiple instances of the module.","A `provider` block to define the AWS provider configuration."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (A `source` argument specifying where the module code is located) is mandatory, as it tells Terraform how to find and download the module. Option B (A unique name for the module instance within the configuration) is also mandatory, as it allows you to reference this specific instance of the module (e.g., `module.my_vpc`). Option C (A `version` argument to specify the Terraform CLI version compatibility) is incorrect; the `version` argument in a `module` block specifies the module''s *own* version, not the Terraform CLI version. CLI version compatibility is handled by the `terraform` block. Option D (An `alias` attribute to create multiple instances of the module) is incorrect; `count` or `for_each` meta-arguments are used for multiple instances. Option E (A `provider` block to define the AWS provider configuration) is typically defined in the root module or passed implicitly/explicitly to the child module, not an essential component of the `module` *call* block itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  95,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_3_56',
  'When calling a child module in a Terraform configuration, which two types of arguments are typically passed within the `module` block? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Values for the child module''s declared `variable` blocks.","The `source` path or address to the child module.","The child module''s `output` values, which are consumed by the parent.","The `provider` configuration specifically for resources within the child module.","The `terraform` block settings, such as required providers, for the child module."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is fundamental for configuring a child module; values are passed as arguments in the `module` block, corresponding to the child module''s `variable` definitions. Option B (correct) is mandatory for any `module` block, specifying where Terraform can find the module code. Option C (incorrect) represents data flowing *out* of the child module, not arguments passed *into* it. Option D (incorrect) can be passed, but it''s less ''typical'' as providers are often inherited; the primary arguments are source and variable values. Option E (incorrect) defines global settings for Terraform itself, not arguments passed to a specific module instance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  96,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_3_57',
  'Which two statements accurately describe how a root module can access data exposed by a child module? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["The child module must explicitly define `output` blocks for the data it intends to expose.","The root module references the output using the syntax `module.<module_name>.<output_name>`.","The root module must define matching `variable` blocks for each of the child module''s outputs.","The child module''s outputs are automatically available as local values in the root module.","The root module can only access outputs if the child module is sourced from the Terraform Registry."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is fundamental; `output` blocks are the mechanism by which a child module declares what data it makes available to its parent. Option B (correct) provides the precise syntax for how the root module (or any calling module) references an output from a child module. Option C (incorrect) is false; `variable` blocks are for inputs, not for consuming outputs. Option D (incorrect) is false; outputs must be explicitly referenced, they do not automatically become local values. Option E (incorrect) is false; outputs can be accessed regardless of whether the module is local, from a Git repository, or from the Terraform Registry.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  97,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_3_58',
  'A Terraform configuration defines a module named `network` that creates an Amazon VPC and subnets, exposing the VPC ID as an output `vpc_id`. Another module, `ec2_instances`, needs this VPC ID as an input. Which two code snippets correctly demonstrate how the `ec2_instances` module can receive this VPC ID? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["```hcl\nmodule \"ec2_instances\" {\n  source = \"./modules/ec2\"\n  vpc_id = module.network.vpc_id\n}\n```","```hcl\nlocals {\n  network_vpc_id = module.network.vpc_id\n}\nmodule \"ec2_instances\" {\n  source = \"./modules/ec2\"\n  vpc_id = local.network_vpc_id\n}\n```","```hcl\nmodule \"ec2_instances\" {\n  source = \"./modules/ec2\"\n  vpc_id = var.network.vpc_id\n}\n```","```hcl\nmodule \"ec2_instances\" {\n  source = \"./modules/ec2\"\n  vpc_id = data.terraform_remote_state.network.outputs.vpc_id\n}\n```","```hcl\nmodule \"ec2_instances\" {\n  source = \"./modules/ec2\"\n  vpc_id = output.network.vpc_id\n}\n```"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) demonstrates the direct and most common way to pass an output from one module (`network`) as an input to another module (`ec2_instances`). Option B (correct) shows another valid and often used pattern: storing the output of one module in a `local` value within the root module, and then passing that `local` value to another module. This can improve readability and centralize values. Option C (incorrect) uses `var.network.vpc_id`, which implies `network` is a variable, not a module, and is incorrect syntax for referencing a module''s output. Option D (incorrect) uses `data.terraform_remote_state`, which is for accessing outputs from a *separate* Terraform state, not directly from another module within the same configuration. Option E (incorrect) uses `output.network.vpc_id`, which is incorrect syntax for referencing a module''s output; `module.` is required.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  98,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_3_59',
  'What are the key characteristics of a child module in a Terraform configuration? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It is called and instantiated by a root module or another child module.","It encapsulates a set of related resources and configurations into a reusable unit.","It cannot define its own `variable` or `output` blocks, relying solely on the parent.","It must always be sourced from the public Terraform Registry.","It directly applies changes to the infrastructure without any interaction from a parent module."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) defines a child module''s role as being an instance of a module called from a parent configuration. Option B (correct) highlights the core purpose of modules: to group resources and logic for reusability and organization. Option C (incorrect) is false; child modules typically define their own `variable` blocks for inputs and `output` blocks to expose data. Option D (incorrect) is false; child modules can be sourced locally, from Git, S3, or private registries, not just the public Terraform Registry. Option E (incorrect) is false; child modules are part of a larger configuration managed by a parent module, and their changes are applied as part of the parent''s `terraform apply` operation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  99,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_3',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_73_m_73_3_60',
  'You have a child module that creates an Amazon S3 bucket and exposes its `bucket_domain_name` as an output. In your root module, you want to use this domain name to configure a CloudFront distribution. Which two methods correctly access this output from the child module? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Reference it directly using `module.<module_name>.<output_name>` (e.g., `module.s3_bucket.bucket_domain_name`).","Assign the module output to a `local` value in the root module, then reference the `local` value (e.g., `local.s3_domain_name`).","Define a `data` source in the root module to fetch the child module''s output.","Define a `variable` in the root module with the same name as the output to capture its value.","Use the `terraform output` command to get the value and then hardcode it into the configuration."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is the standard and most direct way to access an output from a child module within the same Terraform configuration. Option B (correct) is a common and valid pattern for improving readability and organization, especially when an output is used in multiple places within the root module. Option C (incorrect) is typically used for accessing outputs from *remote* Terraform states, not from child modules within the same state. Option D (incorrect) is false; `variable` blocks are for inputs, not for capturing outputs from other modules. Option E (incorrect) is an anti-pattern; hardcoding values defeats the purpose of Infrastructure as Code and makes the configuration non-dynamic and difficult to maintain.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain73_16',
  NOW(),
  100,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_73_3',
  'medium'
);

