INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_36',
  'Which of the following Terraform commands can help detect resource drift? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["`terraform init`","`terraform validate`","`terraform refresh`","`terraform plan`","`terraform fmt`"]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (`terraform refresh`) is specifically designed to reconcile the state file with the actual infrastructure, detecting any drift. Option D (`terraform plan`) implicitly performs a refresh before generating a plan, so it will also show any detected drift as proposed changes. Option A (`terraform init`) initializes the working directory. Option B (`terraform validate`) checks configuration syntax and semantics. Option E (`terraform fmt`) formats configuration files. None of these directly detect drift other than `refresh` and `plan`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_37',
  'After detecting resource drift, which of the following actions can be taken to reconcile the Terraform state with the actual infrastructure? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Manually update the `terraform.tfstate` file to match the infrastructure.","Modify the Terraform configuration to match the drifted infrastructure, then run `terraform apply`.","Run `terraform apply` to revert the infrastructure to match the configuration and state.","Execute `terraform destroy` to remove all resources and recreate them.","Use `terraform import` to bring the drifted resources back into state."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Modify the Terraform configuration to match the drifted infrastructure, then run `terraform apply`) is one valid approach if the manual change was intentional and desired. Terraform will then update the state and infrastructure (if needed) to match the new configuration. Option C (Run `terraform apply` to revert the infrastructure to match the configuration and state) is the other common approach, especially if the drift was unintentional. Terraform will apply changes to bring the infrastructure back in line with the configuration and state. Option A (Manually update the `terraform.tfstate` file to match the infrastructure) is highly discouraged and error-prone. Option D (Execute `terraform destroy` to remove all resources and recreate them) is an extreme measure and not a typical reconciliation step. Option E (Use `terraform import` to bring the drifted resources back into state) is for bringing *unmanaged* resources into state, not for reconciling drift of *managed* resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_38',
  'Which of the following scenarios are examples of resource drift? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["A developer manually changes an EC2 instance type in the AWS Console.","A new resource is added to the Terraform configuration file.","An S3 bucket policy is modified directly via the AWS CLI.","A Terraform provider is updated to a newer version.","The `terraform.tfstate` file is moved to a new remote backend."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (A developer manually changes an EC2 instance type in the AWS Console) is a classic example of drift, as the actual infrastructure (EC2 instance type) no longer matches what''s recorded in the Terraform state. Option C (An S3 bucket policy is modified directly via the AWS CLI) is another clear example of drift, as the policy defined in the Terraform state is now different from the live resource. Option B (A new resource is added to the Terraform configuration file) is a planned change, not drift. Option D (A Terraform provider is updated to a newer version) is a change in the development environment, not the infrastructure itself. Option E (The `terraform.tfstate` file is moved to a new remote backend) is a state management operation, not drift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_39',
  'Which of the following commands would you use to update the Terraform state file with the actual current state of the infrastructure without making any changes to the infrastructure itself? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["`terraform apply -refresh-only`","`terraform refresh`","`terraform plan -refresh=false`","`terraform state push`","`terraform import`"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option B (`terraform refresh`) explicitly updates the state file to reflect the real-world infrastructure without applying any configuration changes. Option A (`terraform apply -refresh-only`) is a newer command that performs a refresh and then updates the state file, effectively achieving the same goal as `terraform refresh` but with the `apply` workflow. Option C (`terraform plan -refresh=false`) explicitly prevents a refresh, so it would not detect drift. Option D (`terraform state push`) is for manually pushing a local state file to a remote backend. Option E (`terraform import`) is for bringing existing infrastructure into Terraform management, not for refreshing existing state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_40',
  'When `terraform plan` detects drift, what does it typically show in its output? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["A list of all resources managed by Terraform","Resources that need to be created or destroyed","Resources that need to be updated to match the configuration","A warning about an outdated provider version","A summary of the cost implications of the changes"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Resources that need to be created or destroyed) would be shown if the configuration has changed or if resources were manually deleted. Option C (Resources that need to be updated to match the configuration) is the primary way drift is displayed; Terraform will show that a resource''s attribute has changed in the real world and proposes to update it back to the configuration''s desired state. Option A (A list of all resources managed by Terraform) is too broad; `plan` focuses on changes. Option D (A warning about an outdated provider version) is a separate warning, not directly drift detection. Option E (A summary of the cost implications of the changes) is a feature of some third-party tools or specific provider configurations, not a standard output of `terraform plan` for drift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_41',
  'Which of the following are valid strategies for handling detected resource drift? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Manually revert the infrastructure changes to match the Terraform state.","Update the Terraform configuration to reflect the manual changes, then apply.","Ignore the drift and proceed with new deployments.","Delete the `terraform.tfstate` file and re-import all resources.","Run `terraform apply -auto-approve` to force the state to match the infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Manually revert the infrastructure changes to match the Terraform state) is a valid strategy if the manual changes were unintentional and the Terraform configuration is still the desired state. Option B (Update the Terraform configuration to reflect the manual changes, then apply) is also a valid strategy if the manual changes were intentional and should become the new desired state. Option C (Ignore the drift and proceed with new deployments) is a bad practice that can lead to unexpected behavior and further inconsistencies. Option D (Delete the `terraform.tfstate` file and re-import all resources) is an extreme and often unnecessary measure. Option E (Run `terraform apply -auto-approve` to force the state to match the infrastructure) is incorrect; `apply` will force the *infrastructure* to match the *state/config*, not the other way around, and `-auto-approve` is for automation, not drift resolution strategy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_42',
  'Which of the following are potential consequences of unmanaged resource drift in a production environment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Increased Terraform execution speed","Unexpected infrastructure behavior or outages","Difficulty in reproducing environments consistently","Automatic rollback of unintended changes","Reduced cloud provider costs"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Unexpected infrastructure behavior or outages) can occur because the actual configuration deviates from the expected configuration, leading to unforeseen interactions or failures. Option C (Difficulty in reproducing environments consistently) is another major consequence, as the Terraform configuration no longer accurately represents the live environment, making it hard to create identical environments. Option A (Increased Terraform execution speed) is incorrect; drift can sometimes slow down operations as Terraform needs to reconcile. Option D (Automatic rollback of unintended changes) is incorrect; Terraform does not automatically roll back changes due to drift. Option E (Reduced cloud provider costs) is unlikely; unmanaged changes could lead to orphaned or over-provisioned resources, potentially increasing costs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_43',
  'When using `terraform plan`, what does the `-out=FILE` option primarily achieve? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It saves the generated plan to a specified file.","It exports the current state file to a new location.","It allows the plan to be reviewed and then applied later using `terraform apply FILE`.","It creates a backup of the configuration files.","It generates a report of all resources that will be destroyed."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (It saves the generated plan to a specified file) is the direct purpose of the `-out` option, allowing the plan to be persisted. Option C (It allows the plan to be reviewed and then applied later using `terraform apply FILE`) is the primary workflow benefit, ensuring that the exact plan reviewed is the one that gets applied, which is crucial for preventing drift between `plan` and `apply` execution. Option B (It exports the current state file to a new location) is incorrect; `terraform state pull` or `terraform state push` handle state file management. Option D (It creates a backup of the configuration files) is incorrect; it saves the plan, not the configuration. Option E (It generates a report of all resources that will be destroyed) is part of the plan output, but not the sole purpose of the `-out` option.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'easy'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_44',
  'Which Terraform commands can be effectively used to detect resource drift by comparing the current state of real-world infrastructure with the Terraform state file? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["`terraform plan`","`terraform refresh`","`terraform apply`","`terraform validate`","`terraform output`"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (`terraform plan`) performs a refresh implicitly and then compares the refreshed state with the configuration, showing any proposed changes (including drift). Option B (`terraform refresh`) explicitly updates the state file to reflect the current real-world infrastructure without making any changes to the infrastructure itself, thus revealing drift. Option C (`terraform apply`) makes changes, it doesn''t just detect. Option D (`terraform validate`) checks configuration syntax. Option E (`terraform output`) displays output values from the state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'medium'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_45',
  'A team is managing a complex AWS environment using Terraform. They have configured an Amazon S3 backend with DynamoDB for state locking. Occasionally, a `terraform apply` operation fails due to network issues, leaving a stale lock in DynamoDB. Which commands are typically used to resolve such a situation and allow subsequent Terraform operations to proceed? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["`terraform refresh`","`terraform state push`","`terraform force-unlock`","`terraform state rm`","`terraform plan -lock=false`"]'::json,
  '{2,4}',
  'Option C (`terraform force-unlock`) is the primary command used to manually remove a stale lock from the remote backend (e.g., DynamoDB). It should be used with extreme caution, only after verifying that no other Terraform operation is actively running. Option E (`terraform plan -lock=false`) is a specific flag that can be used with `terraform plan` (and `apply`) to bypass the state lock. While `plan` typically doesn''t acquire a lock, this flag is available for `apply` as well, but bypassing the lock should be a last resort and only when absolutely certain no other process is modifying the state. Option A (`terraform refresh`) updates the state file with real-world infrastructure, but it does not remove stale locks. Option B (`terraform state push`) is used to manually upload a local state file to a remote backend, not to manage locks. Option D (`terraform state rm`) removes resources from the state file, which is unrelated to unlocking the state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_46',
  'A developer has made manual changes to an Amazon S3 bucket''s configuration directly through the AWS Management Console, bypassing Terraform. The next time they run `terraform plan`, they notice that Terraform proposes to revert these manual changes. Which commands or processes are involved in detecting this ''drift'' between the actual infrastructure and the Terraform state? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["`terraform validate`","`terraform refresh`","`terraform apply`","`terraform state pull`","`terraform plan`"]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Option B (`terraform refresh`) explicitly queries the real-world infrastructure to update the Terraform state file with any out-of-band changes. This command updates the state file without modifying the infrastructure. Option E (`terraform plan`) implicitly performs a refresh operation before comparing the updated state with the desired configuration (from `.tf` files) to identify differences (drift) and propose changes. Option A (`terraform validate`) checks the syntax and configuration of Terraform files but does not interact with the actual infrastructure or state. Option C (`terraform apply`) executes the changes proposed by a plan, but the drift detection happens during the planning phase. Option D (`terraform state pull`) downloads the latest state file from the remote backend to the local machine but does not compare it with the real infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_47',
  'A critical database instance managed by Terraform was accidentally terminated by an engineer directly through the cloud provider''s console. The team needs to restore the database and ensure Terraform''s state reflects the new instance, ideally without re-creating the entire infrastructure. Which Terraform commands or processes are most relevant for recovering from this type of resource drift? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["`terraform destroy`","`terraform refresh`","`terraform import`","`terraform apply`","`terraform state rm`"]'::json,
  '{3,4}',
  'Option D (`terraform apply`) is crucial here. After the database is terminated out-of-band, Terraform''s state still believes it exists. A `terraform plan` (which includes a refresh) would show that the database needs to be created. Running `terraform apply` would then re-create the missing database instance according to the configuration. Option E (`terraform state rm`) can be used to remove the terminated database from Terraform''s state file. This would then allow a subsequent `terraform apply` to create a *new* database instance, effectively restoring it. This is a common approach when a resource is completely gone. Option A (`terraform destroy`) would remove all resources defined in the configuration, which is the opposite of recovery. Option B (`terraform refresh`) would update the state to reflect the database''s absence but wouldn''t re-create it. Option C (`terraform import`) is for bringing *existing* resources under Terraform management, not for re-creating a missing resource from the configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'hard'
);

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_74_m_74_4_48',
  'A team is using Terraform to manage a critical application''s infrastructure. They have a strict policy against manual changes to resources managed by Terraform. However, they suspect some out-of-band changes might have occurred. Which Terraform commands are primarily used to detect and report these configuration drifts without applying any changes? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["`terraform init`","`terraform validate`","`terraform refresh`","`terraform output`","`terraform plan`"]'::json,
  '{2,4}',
  'Option C (`terraform refresh`) explicitly updates the Terraform state file by querying the actual infrastructure. It does not make any changes to the infrastructure itself but updates the state to reflect any out-of-band modifications. Option E (`terraform plan`) implicitly performs a refresh operation as its first step. It then compares the updated state with the desired configuration defined in the `.tf` files and outputs a detailed report of any differences (drift), indicating what changes Terraform would make to bring the infrastructure back into alignment with the configuration. Option A (`terraform init`) initializes a working directory and downloads providers/modules, but does not detect drift. Option B (`terraform validate`) checks the syntax and configuration of Terraform files but does not interact with the actual infrastructure. Option D (`terraform output`) displays output values from the state file but does not detect drift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain74_16',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_74_4',
  'hard'
);

